/* Chapter 6 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":6,"title":"Trading in Islamic Commercial Law","part":"part-ii","partRoman":"II","pages":[129,154],"minutes":65,"estimates":{"f1":65,"f2":70},"difficulty":"Intermediate","difficultyAlt":"intermediate","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch6-1","text":"Define Bai‘ and distinguish trading from loaning, leasing and Shirkah.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch6-2","text":"Explain why trade profit is permitted while interest is prohibited, and rebut two criticisms of Islamic banks’ trading.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch6-3","text":"State the requirements of a valid sale (Figures 6.1 and 6.2) including ownership and possession.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch6-4","text":"Explain the juristic basis for a credit price higher than the cash price.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch6-5","text":"Identify Gharar-based and conditional sales, ‘two bargains in one’ and Bai‘ wal Salaf.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch6-6","text":"Explain ‘Arbun, Bai‘ al Dayn, Bai‘ al ‘Inah, Tawarruq, Hiyal and Bai‘ bil Wafa.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch6-7","text":"Describe the five main types of Khiyar and their use in MPO.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch6-8","text":"Define Bai' and explain how it differs from Riba-based lending in terms of risk, ownership transfer, and reward.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch6-9","text":"Explain the Shari'ah rationale for permitting trade profit while prohibiting interest, including the Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman principle.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch6-10","text":"List the requirements of a valid sale contract, especially conditions on the object of sale (existence, ownership, possession, deliverability).","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch6-11","text":"Explain how cash and credit prices may legitimately differ, and what makes a credit-price arrangement lawful versus Riba-like.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch6-12","text":"Identify Gharar-based prohibited sales and the classical examples used to illustrate them.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch6-13","text":"Explain Bai' al-'Arbun, Bai' al-Dayn, and Bai' al-'Inah, and why each is contested or restricted.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch6-14","text":"Describe the main types of Khiyar (option) available to parties in a sale contract.","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"Most Islamic bank financing is trade-based (Murabaha, Salam, Istisna‘a). The rules of sale in this chapter — ownership before sale, constructive possession, fixed credit price, no sale of debt, no buy-back — are the tests every trade-based product must pass.","whyExpanded":"Since Islamic banks earn income mainly through trading, leasing, and profit-and-loss-sharing rather than lending at interest, the detailed rules of Bai' in this chapter are what make trade-based financing modes like Murabaha, Bai' Mu'ajjal, and Salam Shari'ah-compliant in practice. Understanding exactly where a credit-price markup is legitimate profit versus disguised Riba, and where structures like 'Inah or Bai' al-Dayn cross the line, is essential for evaluating real Islamic banking products and for spotting the difference between a genuine trade transaction and a ruse.","overview":"The chapter defines Bai‘, affirms the legality of trading and its difference from interest, sets out the forms and requirements of a valid sale (object, prices and profit margin, cash and credit prices), then addresses Riba and Gharar in sales, conditional sales, downpayment sales, sale of debt, ‘Inah and legal ruses, and options in sales.","summary":"A valid sale requires free consent, competent parties, a lawful valuable object owned by (or authorised to) the seller, the seller’s ability to deliver, cognisance of the object by examination or description, and a precisely determined price. Non-Thaman goods may be sold for currency on credit; the credit price may exceed the cash price but must be fixed at completion, and the seller cannot be compensated for late payment. Money cannot be traded as a commodity; finance must be supported by genuine trade. Gharar-based sales, Hiyal and Bai‘ al ‘Inah define the limits within which products can be developed, and Khiyar protects parties in sales.","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 6 builds the detailed law of Bai' (trade/sale) that underpins Islamic banks' primary income-generating activity. It establishes why trade profit (risk-based, service-justified) differs fundamentally from Riba (guaranteed, risk-free), sets out the requirements of a valid sale contract -- especially around the object's existence, ownership and possession -- and shows how cash and credit prices can legitimately differ as long as one price is fixed at contract time and never later changed. It then works through the major sources of invalidity in sales: Riba Al-Fadl in same-genus exchanges, Gharar in its many classical and modern (book-out) forms, and prohibited contingent/bundled sales. Finally it examines three contested transaction types -- Bai' al-'Arbun, Bai' al-Dayn, and Bai' al-'Inah/Tawarruq -- and the protective doctrine of Khiyar (options), which safeguards fairness within a genuine sale.","summarySection":"6.13","takeaways":["Bai‘ transfers ownership permanently; trade carries risk and reward.","Sell only what you own and possess (physically or constructively); otherwise promise.","No cap on profit; Ghaban-e-Fahish gives right to rescind.","Credit price may exceed cash price if fixed; never increased for delay.","Book-out contracts, Bai‘ wal Salaf and conditional sales are prohibited.","No sale of debt except face value with recourse; Murabaha rollover prohibited.","‘Inah prohibited; Tawarruq differs; DM uses a time gap.","Khiyar: Shart, Majlis, Ro’yat, ‘Aib, Wasf, Ghaban, Naqad.","Bai' involves complete, instant, irreversible ownership transfer with risk-based return; this is what distinguishes it from Riba's guaranteed, risk-free return on a loan.","Trade profit is legitimized by Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman (risk-bearing) and by the trader's genuine service (seeking, locating, purchasing goods).","A valid sale requires a lawful, existing, owned, deliverable and precisely known object with a fixed, certain price; possession (actual or constructive) is generally required before onward resale.","A credit price may validly exceed the cash price if fixed once at contract time and never later changed; increasing an already-agreed price due to payment delay recreates Riba.","Gharar (not ordinary business risk) invalidates sales -- classical examples (Mulamasah, Munabadhah, Bai' al-Hasat) find modern analogs in book-out/derivative-style contracts.","Bai' al-'Arbun is contested (majority disapprove for Gharar; Hanbalis and modern bodies like AAOIFI permit it with a specified time limit).","Sale of debt (Bai' al-Dayn) is generally prohibited except at face value with recourse to the original debtor, as under Hawalah; genuinely asset-backed Sukuk are an exception since they represent real assets, not pure debt.","Bai' al-'Inah (buy-back between the same parties, netting to disguised interest) is prohibited by the majority; Tawarruq (genuine resale to a different party) is permitted, though viewed by some as Makrooh.","Khiyar (Majlis, Shart, Ro'yat, 'Aib, Wasf, Ghaban) protects fairness within a genuine sale and is fundamentally distinct from speculative conventional financial options."],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you explain why conventional banking is not Bai‘?","Can you justify a trader’s margin and rebut the two criticisms?","Can you reproduce Figures 6.1 and 6.2?","Can you explain constructive possession with the fertiliser example?","Can you explain the juristic basis for credit price > cash price?","Can you distinguish sale of debt and Hawalah?","Can you distinguish ‘Inah, Tawarruq and Bai‘ bil Wafa?","Can you list the types of Khiyar?"],"topics":[{"id":"t6.1","section":"6.1","title":"Introduction: Trade as the Alternative to Riba","pages":[129,129],"tier":"revision","concepts":["bai"],"intuition":"If Islamic banks cannot lend at interest, how do they earn? Mainly by trading, leasing and PLS.","simpleExplanation":"Islam encourages growth of wealth through trade and does not limit profits or fix prices. Islamic banks earn through trading, leasing and PLS. Trade-based modes create debt which the client must repay.","academicExplanation":["Growth of wealth is approved; Islamic law does not limit profits or fix prices but requires avoiding Riba and Gharar so that wealth does not circulate only among the rich (59:7). Islamic banks earn profit through trading, leasing and PLS. Trade-based modes (Murabaha, Mu‘ajjal, Salam, Istisna‘a) create debt, so the finance user must pay back the entire amount (or equivalent in Salam). Any increase termed Riba must be clearly distinguished from growth by trading."],"examExplanation":"Islamic banks earn via trading, leasing, PLS. Trade modes are debt-creating. Distinguish trade profit from Riba.","keyPoints":["Trade-based modes are debt-creating modes of financing.","Trade-based modes (Murabaha, Mu‘ajjal, Salam, Istisna) are debt-creating: the finance user must pay the whole price (or deliver the goods in Salam) like a debt.","Islamic law does not limit profits or fix prices; it removes Riba and Gharar so wealth does not circulate only among the rich (59:7)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Wealth circulating only among the rich","kind":"textbook","text":"The book cites 59:7 on why Islamic law removes Riba and Gharar without capping profits or fixing prices: so that wealth does not circulate only among the rich (p. 129)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t6.3.1","t9.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Through which three areas does the author say Islamic banks earn profit?","options":["Lending, deposits, FX","Trading, leasing and PLS contracts","Insurance, bonds, loans","Charity, Zakat, Waqf"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 129."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t6.2","section":"6.2","title":"Bai‘ — Exchange of Values","pages":[130,131],"tier":"core","concepts":["bai"],"intuition":"Is conventional banking also a kind of Bai‘ (trade)? The author explains why not.","simpleExplanation":"Bai‘ is the exchange of property for property with mutual consent, transferring ownership permanently. Interest-based lending is excluded from valid Bai‘. Trade carries risk and reward; a loan must be repaid equally. Since conventional banks deal in money, their transactions are not Bai‘ in the strict sense.","academicExplanation":["Bai‘ literally means exchange of a subject matter (Mabi‘) for a price (Thaman); the Majallah defines it as exchange of property for property with mutual consent, completed by offer and acceptance. Legally it is giving ownership of a commodity permanently in exchange for the price. In its widest meaning Bai‘ is any bilateral exchange, but exchanges leading to Riba or Gharar are excluded. Prohibited Bai‘ types share Riba, deception or Gharar.","In Pakistan’s Riba review petition it was argued that Bai‘ in 2:275 covers banking; the author replies that the verse gives a general principle — all exchanges are permitted except those involving Riba. Trading exchanges property rights with usufruct; Ijarah (sale of usufruct) retains ownership with the lessor; a loan temporarily but completely transfers ownership, to be returned; Shirkah shares ownership and profit/loss. Bai‘ yields risk-based return while Riba-based lending gives a definite right of return: $1000 lent and $1000 returned is a loan; $1050 required makes it usurious.","When the genera differ, delivery of one item may be delayed (credit sale, Salam); gold or currency for wheat involves no Riba, but wheat for barley with delay does (same genus). Loans must be repaid equally; conventional banks deal in money, so their transactions are not Bai‘ strictly. Sarakhsi: trade is of two kinds — permitted (Bai‘) and prohibited (Riba)."],"examExplanation":"Bai‘ = exchange of property for property with consent; permanent ownership transfer; risk-and-reward. Loan = temporary transfer, equal repayment. Conventional banking (dealing in money) is not Bai‘. Different genera: one side may be deferred.","keyPoints":["All exchanges permitted except those involving Riba (and Gharar).","Risk and reward are inherent in trade.","Contemporary scholars exclude loaning from the meaning of Bai‘."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai‘","definition":"Exchange of property for property with mutual consent, completed by offer and acceptance; gives the buyer permanent ownership against the price."}],"examples":[{"title":"$1,000 lent vs sold","kind":"textbook","text":"If A gives B $1,000 and B returns $1,000, it is a loan; if B must return $1,050 it becomes usurious. In a sale, ownership passes permanently and the buyer owes the agreed price whatever he does with the goods (p. 131)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Because 2:275 uses Bai‘ in a wide sense, conventional banking business counts as Bai‘.","right":"The verse sets the principle that all exchanges are permitted except those involving Riba; banks dealing in money on unequal terms are not engaged in Bai‘ (p. 130)."}],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.1.b","t6.3.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why does the author say conventional banking is not Bai‘ in the strict sense?","options":["Banks are regulated","Banks deal in money, and loan transactions must be repaid equally; unequal exchange is Riba","Banks do not use contracts","Banks deal in goods"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 131."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch6-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch6-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"6.2","sectionTitle":"Bai' -- Exchange of Values","title":"Bai' as Exchange of Values, and Why It Differs from Riba","covers":["t6.2"],"overview":"Bai' is the exchange of property for property (Mabi' for Thaman) with mutual consent; it involves complete, instant, irreversible ownership transfer and risk-based return, which is what distinguishes it from Riba-based lending.","simpleExplanation":"Bai' just means a trade -- you give up ownership of something and get something else (usually money) in return, permanently. In a loan, by contrast, you temporarily hand over money or an item and get the exact same thing back later. Trade profit is fair because the trader takes on real risk (goods could be damaged, prices could fall); interest is unfair because the lender is guaranteed a return no matter what happens to the borrower.","academicExplanation":"Literally, Bai' means the exchange of one thing for another -- the subject matter (Mabi') for the price (Thaman); the Majallah defines a sale as 'the exchange of property for property' completed by declaration and acceptance. In its widest sense, Bai' covers any bilateral exchange contract, but any exchange amounting to Riba is excluded from valid Bai' by jurist consensus, as are Gharar-based exchanges. Ayub rebuts the argument (raised in Pakistan's Supreme Court Riba case) that conventional banking is a form of Bai' under the Qur'an's broad usage of the term: the verse (2:275) actually establishes a general principle of permissibility for exchanges except those involving Riba, not a blanket sanction for banking. Structurally, trade differs from Ijarah (sale of usufruct, where the lessor retains ownership) and from loans (temporary, complete transfer of ownership requiring repayment of a similar item/amount): in a trade, ownership transfers to the buyer completely, instantly, and irreversibly upon the sale agreement, regardless of whether payment is cash or deferred -- in the deferred case, the buyer owes the agreed price (a debt), not the commodity. Riba-based lending guarantees the lender a definite return regardless of outcome; Bai' instead yields a risk-based return -- 'risk and reward' is inherent in trade. Citing Sarakhsi, the chapter notes that trade is of two kinds: permitted (Halal), called Bai' in law, and prohibited (Haram), called Riba -- distinguished explicitly by the Qur'an ('Allah has permitted sale and prohibited Riba'). Because present-day money is fiat, not bullion with intrinsic value, contemporary scholars do not include loaning within the meaning of Bai'.","examExplanation":"Bai' = exchange of Mabi' (subject matter) for Thaman (price), by mutual consent, with complete/instant/irreversible ownership transfer. Distinguish from Ijarah (usufruct only, ownership stays with lessor) and loans (temporary ownership transfer, similar item/amount must be repaid). Key distinguishing feature from Riba: Bai' yields risk-based return (seller bears storage/transit/market/price risk); Riba guarantees a definite return regardless of risk. Sarakhsi: trade = Halal (Bai') or Haram (Riba), both are 'trade' but the Qur'an explicitly distinguishes them.","keyPoints":["Bai' = exchange of property (Mabi') for price (Thaman), by mutual consent, via declaration (offer) and acceptance.","Any exchange amounting to Riba or involving Gharar is excluded from the definition of valid Bai'.","In trade, ownership transfers to the buyer completely, instantly and irreversibly upon the sale agreement -- whether payment is cash or deferred.","In a credit sale, the buyer owes the agreed price (a debt), not the commodity itself, once the sale is concluded.","Ijarah differs from Bai': the lessor retains ownership and its associated liabilities, transferring only usufruct.","A loan differs from Bai': ownership transfers temporarily and a similar item/amount must be repaid; Riba-based lending guarantees a definite return with no business risk to the lender.","Risk-and-reward is inherent in trade; this risk-bearing is what justifies a trader's profit and distinguishes it from Riba's guaranteed, risk-free return.","Because present-day currency is fiat money (not bullion with intrinsic value), contemporary scholars exclude loaning of money from the meaning of Bai'."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai'","definition":"Legally, giving ownership of a commodity to another person in compensation for another commodity (or price); the seller transfers ownership of the commodity to the buyer on a permanent basis in exchange for the price."},{"term":"Mabi'","definition":"The subject matter of a sale -- the commodity being traded."},{"term":"Thaman","definition":"The price -- the monetary counter-value in an exchange."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Risk-and-reward (a trader bears storage, transit, market and price risk) justifies trade profit, unlike Riba's risk-free guaranteed return."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Loan becoming usurious","kind":"textbook","text":"If A gives $1000 to B for use, and B returns exactly $1000, it is a valid (nonusurious) loan; it becomes usurious (Riba) if B must repay any excess, e.g. $1050."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think 'Bai' in its broad Qur'anic usage (business, earning, occupation) automatically legitimizes conventional banking; the chapter explicitly rebuts this -- the verse establishes permissibility of exchange generally, except where Riba is involved, not a blanket cover for interest-based banking."],"distinctions":["Bai' (complete, instant, irreversible ownership transfer; risk-based return) vs Ijarah (usufruct only, ownership stays with lessor) vs Loan (temporary ownership transfer, similar item/amount repaid)","Trade profit (risk-based) vs Riba (guaranteed, risk-free return)"],"relatedConcepts":["Ijarah","Qard","Shirkah","Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.2","pages":[129,130,131]}}]},{"id":"t6.3","section":"6.3","title":"Legality of Trading","pages":[131,132],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["bai"],"intuition":"How strongly does Islam endorse trade?","simpleExplanation":"Trade is a commendable profession. The Qur’an permits trade by mutual consent (4:29) and the Prophet, his Companions and jurists traded. The truthful merchant is praised.","academicExplanation":["Trade is among the commendable lawful sources of earning: “Allah hath allowed trading and prohibited Riba” (2:275); “do not devour your property … except that it be trading by mutual consent” (4:29). The Prophet (pbuh), Companions and Imams traded. Hadiths: carrying firewood to sell is better than begging; the truthful merchant ranks with the Prophets; the best earnings are those of the businessman who does not lie, misappropriate trust, break promises, cavil, boast or delay repayment."],"examExplanation":"Trade permitted and encouraged (2:275; 4:29); honest trader praised.","keyPoints":["“The best type of earning is Bai‘ based on truth and earnings of one by his own hands.”","The Prophet (pbuh), the Companions and the Imams themselves traded.","“The best type of earning is a sale based on truth and one’s own labour.”"],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Selling firewood rather than begging","kind":"textbook","text":"The Prophet (pbuh) said that carrying a load of wood and selling it is better than begging from people — an encouragement to earn through trade (p. 132)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t6.3.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which verse is cited for ‘trading by your mutual consent’?","options":["2:275","4:29","3:130","59:7"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 131."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch6-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch6-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"6.3","sectionTitle":"Legality of Trading; Trade (Profit) versus Interest","title":"Legality of Trading and Why Trade Profit Differs from Interest","covers":["t6.3","t6.3.1"],"overview":"Trading is a commendable, Qur'an-sanctioned profession; the permissibility of trading precedes the prohibition of Riba in the Qur'an, signifying trade as the legitimate alternative, justified by risk-bearing under Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman.","simpleExplanation":"Islam actively encourages trading and even the Prophet and his Companions traded for a living. The order of the Qur'anic verse -- mentioning that trade is allowed before saying interest is banned -- is read as pointing to trade as the proper alternative to interest. A trader's profit is fair because he does real work (finding, buying, storing, transporting goods) and takes on real risk (loss, damage, price swings); a lender earning interest does neither.","academicExplanation":"The Qur'an states 'Allah hath allowed trading and prohibited Riba' (2:275), and trading is described as commendable, undertaken by the Prophet, his Companions and eminent jurists. Hadith praise honest trade highly, ranking the truthful merchant with prophets, the truthful, martyrs and the pious, and praising 'easy' buyers/sellers/debtors/creditors. Regarding trade versus interest (6.3.1), the chapter infers from Qur'an 2:275-276 that Riba on loans/debts must not be equated with trade profit -- notably, permissibility of trading is mentioned before the prohibition of Riba, signifying trade as the alternative. Two common criticisms of Islamic banks' trading activities are addressed and rejected: (1) that banks, as intermediaries, shouldn't engage in trading/real-sector activity -- rejected because banking has never been fixed to one structure, and even conventional banks engage in real business (e.g. German merchant banking); (2) that charging more than the cash price is equivalent to interest -- rejected in detail. The key distinguishing principle is Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman: one can claim profit only if ready to bear business risk; Riba-based transactions violate this because reward is guaranteed to one party while the other bears the risk. A trader's profit margin is justified by (a) providing a definite service -- seeking, locating and purchasing goods for the client -- and (b) bearing business risk (damage in storage/transit, market and price risk) -- justifying a profit margin even where a credit sale's margin exceeds the cash-market margin, provided the once-agreed price is not later changed even if payment is late. By contrast, interest-based earning creates a 'rentier class,' diverting an increasing share of national produce away from those doing real productive work.","examExplanation":"Trade is Qur'an-sanctioned (2:275) and Prophetically practiced/praised; permissibility of trade textually precedes the Riba prohibition, signaling trade as the alternative. Two objections to bank trading rejected: (1) banks-as-intermediaries objection -- rejected, banking structure isn't fixed and even conventional banks do real business; (2) credit-price-equals-interest objection -- rejected via Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman (profit requires risk-bearing) and the trader's definite service + risk-bearing (storage/transit/market/price risk) justification. Interest creates a 'rentier class' diverting reward from real economic work.","keyPoints":["The Qur'an (2:275) explicitly permits trading and prohibits Riba; the Prophet, Companions, and eminent jurists themselves engaged in trade.","Hadith rank the truthful merchant highly (alongside prophets, the truthful, martyrs, and the pious) and praise 'easy' dealing in buying, selling, debt repayment and lending.","The Qur'anic sequencing -- permissibility of trade mentioned before prohibition of Riba -- signifies trade as the legitimate alternative to Riba.","Two criticisms of Islamic banks' trading are rejected: banks-as-intermediaries objection, and the 'credit price = interest' objection.","Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman: profit can be claimed only if the claimant bears business risk/liability; Riba-based transactions violate this since the lender's return is guaranteed regardless of risk.","A trader's profit is justified by (a) a definite service (seeking, locating, purchasing goods) and (b) bearing business risk (storage, transit, market, price risk).","Once a price is mutually agreed at the time of bargain, it cannot be changed later, even if payment is delayed.","Interest-based earning creates a 'rentier class' that captures an increasing share of output without contributing productive work."],"definitions":[{"term":"Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman","definition":"The Shari'ah principle that one can claim profit only if ready to take liability -- to bear business risk, if any; profit is legitimized by engaging in economic activity that contributes to development of resources and society."}],"conditions":["A price, once mutually agreed at the time of bargain, must not be changed later, even if the buyer delays payment."],"principles":["Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman: profit requires bearing corresponding business risk.","The Qur'anic sequencing of permitting trade before prohibiting Riba signals trade as the sanctioned alternative."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"'Urwah's goat trade","kind":"textbook","text":"The Prophet gave a Companion, 'Urwah, one dinar to buy a goat; 'Urwah bought two goats for one dinar, sold one for one dinar, and delivered a goat plus the dinar to the Prophet -- an example the Prophet praised for honesty and trading skill."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume banks acting as intermediaries can't legitimately be traders; the chapter explicitly rejects this, noting banking structure isn't fixed and even conventional banks conduct real business.","Charging a higher credit price is not automatically equivalent to interest -- what matters is whether the price is fixed once and for all at the time of the bargain (see Section 6.5.3 for the detailed treatment)."],"distinctions":["Trade profit (risk-based, tied to a definite service) vs interest/Riba (guaranteed, risk-free 'rent' on money)"],"relatedConcepts":["Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman","Al-Ghunm bil-Ghurm","Murabaha"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.3","pages":[131,132,133]}}]},{"id":"t6.3.1","section":"6.3.1","title":"Trade (Profit) versus Interest: Permissibility versus Prohibition","pages":[132,133],"tier":"core","concepts":["bai","riba","kharaj-daman"],"intuition":"Critics say Islamic banks should not trade, and that a credit price above the cash price is just interest. How does the author respond?","simpleExplanation":"Trade profit comes from real activity where risk is shared; interest guarantees reward to one party. A trader earns a margin for the service of finding and buying goods and for bearing storage, transit, market and price risks — so a higher credit margin is justified, provided the agreed price never changes after the sale.","academicExplanation":["From 2:275–276, Riba must not be equated with trade profit; the mention of trade before Riba signifies trade is the alternative. Two criticisms: (1) banks are intermediaries and should not trade; (2) charging more than the cash price equals interest. Both are invalid: banking has never been static in structure, and with risk management Islamic banks may adopt any Shari’ah-compliant modus operandi — even conventional banks engage in real business (German merchant banking).","Trade profit results from real investment activity with risk spread among parties; Riba guarantees reward to one party, violating Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman. A trader’s margin is justified by (a) the definite service of seeking, locating and purchasing goods, and (b) business risk — damage in storage or transit, market and price risk — even if the credit margin exceeds the cash margin. The price, once agreed, cannot change even if payment is late. Earning money from money creates a rentier class."],"examExplanation":"Trade profit: real activity, shared risk, service + risk-bearing justify margin; price fixed once agreed. Interest: guaranteed reward, violates Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman, creates rentier class. Criticisms (banks shouldn’t trade; credit price = interest) rejected.","keyPoints":["Trade precedes Riba in 2:275 — trade is the alternative.","Two justifications for trader’s margin: service and risk."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Why a trader’s margin is earned","kind":"textbook","text":"A trader finds a machine abroad, buys it, ships it and stores it. He bears transit, storage and price risk until he sells it for 120 against a cost of 100. The 20 rewards his service and risk; a lender of 100 who demands 120 has done neither (pp. 132–133)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Banks are intermediaries, so Islamic banks should not trade in goods.","right":"Banking has never been confined to one structure; even conventional banks engage in real business (German merchant banking). With proper risk management Islamic banks may trade (p. 132)."}],"relatedTopics":["t6.5.3","t17.3.8","t4.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which two things justify a trader’s profit margin according to Section 6.3.1?","options":["Time and inflation","A service (locating/purchasing goods) and bearing business risk","Collateral and guarantees","Government approval"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 132–133."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"Trade profit","b":"Riba","text":"Trade profit comes from real investment with business risk shared among parties; Riba guarantees one party’s reward while the other bears all risk, violating Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman (p. 132)."}],"debate":[{"issue":"Should Islamic banks be traders, and is a higher credit price interest?","criticism":"Banks are intermediaries and should not get involved in trade; charging more than the cash price equals interest-based financing.","response":"Banking has never been restricted to one structure; with proper risk management Islamic banks can adopt any compliant modus operandi (conventional banks also do real business, e.g. German merchant banking). Trade profit is earned by providing a service and bearing risk; the price once agreed is never increased.","takeaway":"Trading that meets all conditions of Islamic commercial law should not be equated with interest."}]},{"id":"t6.4","section":"6.4","title":"Types of Bai‘","pages":[133,134],"tier":"core","concepts":["bai"],"intuition":"How can sales be classified — by legality and by the counter values exchanged?","simpleExplanation":"By legality: valid (Nafiz/Sahih), void (Batil), voidable (Fasid) and suspended (Mawquf). By counter values: absolute sale (Bai‘ al Mutlaq), Sarf (money for money), barter (Muqayaza), credit sale (Mu’ajjal), Salam and Istisna‘a. Figure 6.1 maps these forms.","academicExplanation":["Hanafis categorise sale into Bai‘ Nafiz/Sahih, Batil (no transfer of title, unenforceable) and Fasid (a non-severe violation, enforceable after modification); a sale with title transfer subject to conditions is Mawquf. The most important valid form is exchanging a commodity for money spot or on credit (Bai‘ al Mutlaq). Money-for-money is Bai‘ al Sarf; goods for goods is barter (Bai‘ al Muqayaza). Mu’ajjal defers the price to a definite future time; Salam is immediate payment for future delivery. Figure 6.1 shows forms with respect to counter values: spot/cash, deferred payment (Bai‘ al-Nasia, instalment sale Bai‘ al-Taqseet), trust sales (Tauliyyah at cost, Murabaha — direct, via third-party agent, client as agent, or promise-based three-party — Muhatah at a discount on cost), Musawamah, Salam, Istisna‘a (periodic payment) and barter."],"examExplanation":"Legality: Sahih/Nafiz, Batil, Fasid, Mawquf. Counter values: Mutlaq, Sarf, Muqayaza, Mu’ajjal, Salam, Istisna‘a; trust sales Tauliyyah, Murabaha, Muhatah; Musawamah.","keyPoints":["Bai‘ al Mutlaq is the most important valid type.","Murabaha structures: direct, third-party agent, client as agent, promise-based (3 parties)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Naming the sale","kind":"textbook","text":"Car for cash now: Bai‘ al Mutlaq (spot). Car now, price in six months: Mu’ajjal. Wheat price paid now, wheat delivered in May: Salam. Dollars for rupees: Sarf. Rice for wheat: Muqayaza (barter). At cost: Tawliyah; cost plus profit: Murabaha; at a loss: Wadhi‘ah (pp. 133–134)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t5.7","t5.8","t9.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Exchange of goods for goods is called:","options":["Bai‘ al Sarf","Bai‘ al Muqayaza","Bai‘ Salam","Bai‘ Mu’ajjal"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 133."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch6-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch6-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"6.4","sectionTitle":"Types of Bai'","title":"Types of Bai' by Legality and by Counter-Values","covers":["t6.4"],"overview":"Sales are classified by legal status (valid/Sahih, void/Batil, voidable/Fasid, suspended/Mawquf, as per Chapter 5) and by the nature of the counter-values exchanged (Bai' al-Sarf, Bai' al-Muqayaza, Mu'ajjal, Salam, Bai' al-Mutlaq, etc.).","simpleExplanation":"Sales can be sorted in two ways: by whether they're legally sound (fully valid, totally void, fixable/voidable, or on hold), and by what's being exchanged for what -- money for money, goods for goods (barter), goods now for money later (credit), or money now for goods later (Salam).","academicExplanation":"Reiterating the Hanafi classification from Chapter 5, sale (Bai') can be Nafiz/Sahih (valid and immediately effective), Batil (void -- ownership title never transfers, not Shari'ah-compliant, unenforceable), Fasid (voidable -- involves a non-severe Shari'ah violation, enforceable if modified/cured by mutual agreement), or Mawquf (suspended -- title transfer subject to specific conditions). By counter-values exchanged, the main valid forms are: Bai' al-Mutlaq (exchanging a commodity with money, spot or credit -- the most important/common type); Bai' al-Sarf (exchange of monetary units/money forms -- gold, silver, currencies); Bai' al-Muqayaza (barter -- goods for goods); Bai' Mu'ajjal (deferred price payment to a future, definite time -- credit sale); and Bai' Salam (immediate payment against future delivery of the commodity). Each type carries its own specific validating rules.","examExplanation":"Legal-status classification (from Ch.5): Sahih/Nafiz (valid), Batil (void, no ownership transfer), Fasid (voidable, curable), Mawquf (suspended). Counter-value classification: Bai' al-Mutlaq (goods for money, spot/credit -- most common), Bai' al-Sarf (money-for-money), Bai' al-Muqayaza (barter), Bai' Mu'ajjal (deferred payment/credit sale), Bai' Salam (immediate payment, deferred delivery).","keyPoints":["By legal status: Sahih/Nafiz (valid), Batil (void -- ownership never transfers), Fasid (voidable -- curable by removing the defect), Mawquf (suspended pending a condition).","Bai' al-Mutlaq (goods exchanged for money, spot or credit) is described as the most important type of valid Bai'.","Bai' al-Sarf is the exchange of monetary units/money forms (gold, silver, currencies) against each other.","Bai' al-Muqayaza is barter -- goods exchanged directly for goods.","Bai' Mu'ajjal defers the price payment to a future, definite time (credit sale).","Bai' Salam is the reverse of credit sale: immediate (advance) payment against future delivery of the commodity."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai' al-Mutlaq","definition":"The normal sale of goods for money, on a spot or credit basis; also called absolute sale."},{"term":"Bai' al-Sarf","definition":"The exchange of money or monetary units (gold, silver, currencies) on both sides of the transaction."},{"term":"Bai' al-Muqayaza (Muqayadhah)","definition":"Barter sale -- exchange of goods for goods."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think 'sale' means only cash-for-goods; the chapter's classification by counter-values shows Bai' spans barter, currency exchange, credit sale, and forward (Salam) sale, each with distinct governing rules."],"distinctions":["Bai' Mu'ajjal (deferred price, immediate delivery -- credit sale) vs Bai' Salam (immediate price, deferred delivery -- forward sale)"],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al-Sarf","Bai' Salam","Istisna'a","Valid/Fasid/Batil/Mawquf contracts"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.4","pages":[133]}}],"table":{"caption":"Main forms of Bai‘ (p. 133)","head":["Form","Meaning"],"rows":[["Bai‘ al Mutlaq","Goods for money, spot or credit"],["Bai‘ al Sarf","Money for money (gold, silver, currencies)"],["Bai‘ al Muqayaza","Barter — goods for goods"],["Bai‘ Mu’ajjal","Price deferred to a definite time"],["Bai‘ Salam","Price now, goods later"]]}},{"id":"t6.5","section":"6.5","title":"Requirements of a Valid Sale Contract","pages":[133,135],"tier":"core","concepts":["bai"],"intuition":"What does a valid sale look like in one picture? Figure 6.2 summarises it.","simpleExplanation":"A valid sale needs proper offer and acceptance, free consent, lawful goods and medium of exchange, record-keeping, security, full discharge of dues and fulfilment of promises. It must be prompt and absolute — not future-dated or contingent. Figure 6.2: the subject matter must be existent, valuable, usable, owned by the seller with risk, and delivered physically or constructively; the price must be certain and known.","academicExplanation":["The rules pertain to offer and acceptance, free consent, legality of wares and medium of exchange, record-keeping, security, discharging dues in full and fulfilling promises. A sale attributed to a future date or contingent on a future event is void/voidable and must be executed afresh. Approved forms reflect mutual consent and justice with good manners, leniency and honesty; justice includes fulfilling promises and contracts, correct weights and measures, and clear price. The parties must have understanding and discretion; the commodity and price must be known; the commodity must have legal value and exist or be deliverable immediately or later (the latter specific to Salam)."],"examExplanation":"Valid Bai‘ (Fig. 6.2): subject matter existent/existable, valuable, usable, seller’s ownership and risk, delivery (physical Haqiqi or constructive Hukmi); price certain (Muta’ayyan) and known (Malum). Sale must be prompt and absolute.","keyPoints":["Future-dated or contingent sale must be executed afresh.","A sale must be prompt and absolute; a sale attributed to a future date or contingent on a future event is void/voidable and must be executed afresh.","Mutual consent requires volition and truthfulness; justice requires correct weights, clear price and honesty."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"A contingent sale","kind":"practice","text":"“I sell you my house for 5 million if I get my transfer abroad next month.” This sale is contingent on a future event, so it is void or voidable and must be executed afresh when the condition is met (pp. 133–135)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t6.5.1","t5.4.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to Figure 6.2, delivery (Qabza) may be:","options":["Only physical","Physical (Haqiqi) or constructive (Hukmi)","Only by documents","Not required"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 135."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch6-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch6-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"6.5","sectionTitle":"Requirements of a Valid Sale Contract; The Object of the Sale Contract","title":"Requirements of a Valid Sale: Consent, Object, Existence, Ownership and Possession","covers":["t6.5","t6.5.1","t6.5.2","t6.5.3"],"overview":"A valid sale requires free consent, competent parties, a lawful/existing/owned/deliverable object precisely known to both parties, and (per the majority) possession by the seller before onward resale.","simpleExplanation":"For a sale to be valid, both sides must genuinely agree, be capable adults, and both must know exactly what's being sold and its price. The thing being sold must be clean and lawful (no wine, pork, or dead-animal remains), must actually exist and be owned by the seller at the time of the deal, and the seller generally needs to have taken possession of it (physically or in a way where it's effectively under their control) before reselling it. You can't sell something you don't yet own, though you can promise to sell it once you do.","academicExplanation":"A valid sale requires proper offer and acceptance, free consent of both parties (volition, truthfulness, absence of coercion/fraud/lying), legality of the goods and any exchange medium, and must be prompt/absolute (not attributed to a future date or made contingent, else it's void/voidable and must be re-executed). Mutual consent, justice (fulfilment of promises, correct weights/measures, clear price/wage stipulation, honesty), and good manners (politeness, forgiveness, compensation, hardship removal) are the approved sale's underlying principles. Regarding the object (Mabi'): (1) it must be pure, lawful (Mubah), clean, wholesome, marketable, and Mal-e-Mutaqawam (commercial value) -- wine, pork, and flesh/bones of non-ritually-slaughtered animals cannot be sold; (2) it must exist at contract time and the seller must be its real owner -- selling something you merely hope to acquire (e.g. a bank selling a car it hopes to later buy from a factory) is void, though a promise to sell is permitted; (3) possession before resale is required by the majority (Shafi'e, Muhammad, and others), based on Ibn Umar's report that people were punished for selling foodstuff before carrying it home -- though Hanafis and others hold that mere presence and full knowledge of the item suffices, and Majallah confirms delivery is complete when the seller sets the item aside for the buyer, giving the buyer unrestricted access (constructive/Hukmi possession). If A buys a car from B but B hasn't delivered it (actually or constructively), A cannot resell it to C -- such a resale is void. Existence-at-contract-time is relaxed only for Salam and Istisna'a (future supply/manufacture, defined and possible, avoiding Gharar) -- e.g. selling an unborn calf is void for Gharar, but a promise to deliver/manufacture a defined future good is valid. (4) The object must be capable of certain delivery -- a fled camel, a bird in the air, a fish in water, or a stolen vehicle cannot be sold; jurists link this to title (ownership must be real and transfer rights legal) and to avoiding conflict/unjustified profit from uncertain contracts. Sale of a debt against another debt, sale of what one does not possess, and sale by a buyer of what he's bought before taking possession are all examples of undeliverable-object prohibitions; Ibn Taymiyah clarifies the real cause is Gharar, not nonexistence per se. Constructive possession suffices for onward resale in the Hanafi and Majallah view (e.g. a Karachi bank buying fertilizer set aside for it in a Lahore factory becomes the rightful, risk-bearing owner and may resell it). Notably, a buyer who purchased on credit (e.g. via Murabaha) and has taken possession, but not yet paid in full, may still validly resell the asset -- this is not 'selling what he doesn't own.'","examExplanation":"Valid sale requires: free consent, competent parties, lawful/pure/existing/owned/deliverable/precisely-known object, and (majority view) seller's possession (actual or constructive/Hukmi) before resale. Object conditions: (1) pure/lawful/Mal-e-Mutaqawam (no wine, pork, non-Halal animal remains); (2) must exist and be owned by seller at contract time (exception: Salam/Istisna'a for defined future goods); (3) possession (actual or Hukmi) required before onward sale -- majority view, though Hanafis stress presence+knowledge over physical possession; (4) must be capable of certain delivery (no fled camel/bird in air/fish in water/stolen car) -- the real underlying cause of these prohibitions is Gharar (per Ibn Taymiyah), not nonexistence itself. A Murabaha buyer who has taken possession but not fully paid can still validly resell -- not 'selling what he doesn't own.'","keyPoints":["A valid sale requires free mutual consent, competent parties, and must be prompt/absolute -- not attributed to a future date or made contingent.","The object must be pure, lawful (Mubah), clean, and Mal-e-Mutaqawam (of commercial value); wine, pork, idols, and non-Halal-slaughtered animal remains cannot be sold.","The object must exist at contract time and the seller must be its real owner -- you cannot sell what you merely hope to later acquire, though you may promise to sell it.","The majority view requires possession (actual or constructive/Hukmi) by the seller before onward resale; Hanafis emphasize presence and full knowledge of the item as sufficient.","Constructive (Hukmi) possession exists when the seller has set the item aside for the buyer with unrestricted access, even without physical transfer.","The object must be capable of certain delivery -- items like a fled camel, a bird in the air, a stolen vehicle, or debt-for-debt cannot be validly sold.","Ibn Taymiyah clarifies: nonexistent items are prohibited from sale not because they're nonexistent per se, but because of the Gharar this typically involves.","Salam and Istisna'a are the sanctioned exceptions permitting sale of a not-yet-existing but precisely defined future good.","A buyer who has taken possession of a credit-purchased asset (even without full payment) may validly resell it -- this is not 'selling what he doesn't own.'"],"definitions":[{"term":"Mal-e-Mutaqawam","definition":"Wealth/property having a commercial value, the use of which is lawful under the Shari'ah."},{"term":"Qabza (Haqiqi vs Hukmi)","definition":"Delivery/possession -- either physical (Haqiqi) or constructive (Hukmi, where the item is set aside for the buyer with unrestricted access, though not physically transferred)."}],"conditions":["The object of sale must be pure, lawful, clean, marketable, and of commercial value (Mal-e-Mutaqawam).","The object must exist at the time of the contract and the seller must own it (except under Salam/Istisna'a).","The seller should have possession (actual or constructive) of the object before reselling it onward, per the majority view.","The object must be capable of certain delivery -- known, deliverable, and free of excessive uncertainty."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Selling a car the bank does not yet own","kind":"textbook","text":"If a bank sells client C a car currently owned by factory F, hoping to buy it from F and deliver it later, the sale is void per Shari'ah; at most the bank can make a promise to sell."},{"title":"Constructive possession of fertilizer","kind":"textbook","text":"A Karachi-based bank contracts to buy 100,000 bags of fertilizer from a Lahore factory; the factory sets the bags aside and gives constructive possession to the bank. The bank is the rightful owner, bears the asset/market/price risk, and may resell the fertilizer to a third party."},{"title":"Reselling a partly-paid Murabaha car","kind":"textbook","text":"Client C buys a car via Murabaha, payable over five years, and owns it from day one. After one year, C may sell the car to Y (who takes over the remaining instalments), even though C hasn't paid all instalments -- this is not selling what he doesn't own."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think physical possession is always mandatory before resale; many jurists (including Hanafis) hold that presence and full knowledge of the item is enough, and constructive (Hukmi) possession -- where risk/control have passed even without physical transfer -- suffices for a valid onward sale.","The prohibition on selling nonexistent items is often misunderstood as absolute; Ibn Taymiyah's view (endorsed in the text) is that the real underlying reason is Gharar, which is why Salam and Istisna'a can validly involve not-yet-existing goods once Gharar is removed by precise specification."],"distinctions":["Physical (Haqiqi) possession vs constructive (Hukmi) possession -- both can satisfy the possession requirement per the Hanafi/Majallah view","Selling what one merely hopes to acquire (void) vs promising to sell/buy it later (valid as Wa'dah)"],"relatedConcepts":["Salam","Istisna'a","Gharar","Murabaha","Wa'dah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5","pages":[133,134,135,136,137,138]}}],"table":{"caption":"Figure 6.2 — Elements of valid Bai‘ (reconstructed)","head":["Element","Requirements"],"rows":[["Subject matter (Mabi‘)","Existent/existable; valuable; usable (not prohibited); in seller’s ownership and risk"],["Price (Thaman)","Certain (Muta’ayyan); known (Malum)"],["Delivery/conveyance (Qabza)","Physical (Haqiqi) or constructive (Hukmi)"]]}},{"id":"t6.5.1","section":"6.5.1","title":"The Object of the Sale Contract","pages":[135,138],"tier":"core","concepts":["qabd","ownership"],"intuition":"Can a bank sell a car it has not yet bought from the factory? No — at most it can promise to sell.","simpleExplanation":"The object must be lawful and of commercial value, exist, be owned by the seller and be possessed (physically or constructively) before resale, and be capable of certain delivery. A bank that doesn’t yet own the car can only promise to sell. Constructive possession — goods set aside at the seller’s risk and free access for the buyer — is enough.","academicExplanation":["Conditions: (1) the object must be pure, lawful (Mubah), marketable with legal value — Mal-e-Mutaqawam; no wine, pork, carrion or idols. (2) It must exist and the vendor must own it: a bank selling client C a car still owned by factory F, hoping to buy it later, is void — at most a promise to sell. (3) Possession before sale: Ibn Umar reported people being punished for reselling foodstuff before carrying it home; Shafi‘i and others require possession, while many (including Hanafis) hold it sufficient that the item is present and fully known; the Majallah says delivery is complete when set aside for the buyer with no restriction. If A buys a car from B that is not delivered to A or his agent, A’s resale to C is void.","Existence is relaxed for Salam and Istisna‘a: a particular thing must exist, but a promise to deliver or manufacture defined goods need not, if possible and definite. (4) The object must be capable of certain delivery — no fled camel, bird in air, fish in water, stolen car until found. Sale of debt for debt, of what one does not possess, and of what may or may not exist (a she-camel’s offspring) are void. Ibn Taymiyah: nonexistence per se is not the cause; Gharar is (e.g. Mua‘awamah — selling fruit for years ahead). “Do not sell what you do not possess” refers to inability to deliver.","Rationale: the seller should bear the risk and reward. Salam goods: the Hadith against transferring Salam goods before possession is weak; Parallel Salam is allowed. Ibn Hazm: what one owns is as if possessed even if in India. Example: a car in a garage to which A has free access is in A’s constructive possession; a Karachi bank buying 100,000 fertiliser bags set aside by a Lahore factory owns them and can sell them, bearing asset, market and price risk until sold. One may promise to sell what one does not yet own; the sale must be executed after possession."],"examExplanation":"Object: lawful, valuable (Mal-e-Mutaqawam), existent, owned, possessed (physical or constructive) before resale, deliverable. Bank can only promise before owning. Constructive possession = set aside, buyer’s risk and free access. Salam/Istisna‘a exceptions for defined future goods.","keyPoints":["Sale before ownership is void; promise is allowed.","Resale before (actual or constructive) delivery is void.","Ibn Taymiyah: Gharar, not non-existence, is the cause.","Fertiliser example: constructive possession across cities."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Fertiliser in Lahore, bank in Karachi","kind":"textbook","text":"A Karachi-based bank buys 100,000 bags of fertiliser from a Lahore factory. Once the factory sets them aside and gives constructive possession, the bank owns them, can sell them to a third party, and bears asset, market and price risk until it sells."},{"title":"Car still at the factory","kind":"textbook","text":"If a bank sells client C a car still owned by factory F, hoping to buy it later, the sale is void; the bank can only promise to sell."},{"title":"Fertiliser in Lahore, bank in Karachi","kind":"textbook","text":"A Karachi bank buys 100,000 bags from a Lahore factory that sets them aside and gives constructive possession. The bank owns them, bears their risk and may sell them on (p. 138)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Physical delivery is always required before resale.","right":"Constructive possession — the goods are at the buyer’s risk and he has free access to take delivery — suffices (Hanafi and Majallah view)."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.4.2","t9.6","t10.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A has bought a car from B, who has parked it in a garage where A has free access and may take delivery whenever he wishes. A sells it to C. The sale is:","options":["Void — no physical possession","Valid — A has constructive possession","Voidable","Riba"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 138."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t6.5.2","section":"6.5.2","title":"Prices and the Profit Margin","pages":[138,139],"tier":"core","concepts":["fair-pricing"],"intuition":"Is there a maximum profit rate in Islamic trade?","simpleExplanation":"Islam leaves prices and margins to demand and supply, with transparency and honesty. The OIC Fiqh Council sets no limit on profit percentage (subject to ethics). Ghaban-e-Fahish (hidden overcharging) gives the buyer the right to rescind. Some texts mention guideline maxima (5% wares, 10% animals, 20% real estate).","academicExplanation":["Islam does not fix prices or profit margins, leaving them to demand and supply; the Prophet allowed the competitive price mechanism. The State may intervene only for moral, religious or cultural norms (footnote: prices may be fixed in consultation with experts if suppliers act injudiciously). ‘Urwah’s purchase of two goats for one dinar and resale of one for a dinar — the Prophet praised his honesty and expertise. OIC Fiqh Council (5th session): freedom to buy and sell; no restriction on profit percentage, with ethics of moderation and leniency; avoid fraud, deceit and monopoly; government fixes prices only when artificial factors distort the market. Ghaban-e-Fahish — charging an exorbitant price while implying market price — gives the buyer the option to rescind. Some books infer maximum profit rates of 5% for wares, 10% for animals and 20% for real estate (Majallah, Art. 165)."],"examExplanation":"No cap on profit % (OIC Fiqh Council); market-determined with ethics. Ghaban-e-Fahish → buyer may rescind. Inferred guideline maxima: 5% wares, 10% animals, 20% real estate.","keyPoints":["‘Urwah’s two goats illustrate honest, skilled trading.","Inferred maxima are not generally recommended rates."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"‘Urwah and the goats","kind":"textbook","text":"Sent with one dinar to buy a goat, ‘Urwah bought two, sold one for a dinar and returned with a goat and the dinar. The Prophet (pbuh) prayed for his trade — honest profit is praised (p. 138)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islamic law fixes a maximum profit rate.","right":"The OIC Fiqh Council says there is no restriction; only inferences in the Majallah (5% wares, 10% animals, 20% real estate) exist, and Ghaban-e-Fahish gives the buyer an option to rescind (p. 139)."}],"relatedTopics":["t3.3.4","t4.2.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"If a buyer later learns he was charged an exorbitant price presented as the market price, he:","options":["Has no remedy","Has the option to rescind and take back his money","Must pay a penalty","Must accept a partial refund only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 139."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch6-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch6-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"6.5.2","sectionTitle":"Prices and the Profit Margin; Cash and Credit Prices","title":"Pricing Freedom, Profit Margins, and the Legitimacy of Differing Cash/Credit Prices","covers":["t6.5.2"],"overview":"Islamic law generally leaves prices and profit margins to market forces, subject to avoiding excessive profiteering (Ghaban-e-Fahish); a credit price may legitimately exceed the cash price provided one definite price is fixed at the time of contracting and never changed thereafter.","simpleExplanation":"Islam doesn't set price caps or fixed profit percentages -- prices are left to buyers and sellers to negotiate, as long as no one is cheated or misled about the market price. A seller can charge more if you pay later than if you pay now, as long as you both agree on one clear final price when you make the deal -- what's not allowed is agreeing to a price and then bumping it up later just because payment is late, or leaving the price open/uncertain based on how long you take to pay.","academicExplanation":"As a principle, Islam does not fix prices or profit margins, trusting demand-and-supply dynamics for economic justice and efficient resource allocation, subject only to moral/religious/cultural limits with the State stepping in only where market abuse (e.g. monopoly, artificial price distortion) occurs -- per the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council's four resolved points (freedom to trade within Shari'ah, no restriction on profit percentage, avoidance of fraud/cheating/deceit/monopoly, and government price-fixing reserved for clear market failure). What is prohibited is Ghaban-e-Fahish (excessive profiteering with deception) -- charging an exorbitant price while implying the market price is being charged; a defrauded buyer may rescind and reclaim his money. Some classical books suggest indicative maximum profit rates (5% on wares, 10% on animals, 20% on real estate), though jurists generally don't mandate specific rates. On cash versus credit prices (6.5.3): most jurists agree a seller may quote two prices (cash and credit) as long as one is settled within the same meeting, provided the practice is normal in the market, aims at genuine trade, and avoids Ghaban-e-Fahish -- based on the Hadith 'the person who makes two bargains in one sale, the lower of the two is lawful for him, or he would be charging Riba,' interpreted (per Semak, Aozaii et al.) as requiring one price to be fixed before separation. Where only a single (higher) credit price is quoted and accepted, jurists differ: some (fearing implicit Riba if the buyer perceives the excess as a time-charge) disapprove, but the majority (mainly Shafi'e and Hanafi schools) deem it lawful because the seller stipulates the whole price at the time of bargain for the item, not as a reward for time per se -- provided that once stipulated, the price is never later changed. Multiple classical citations (Tirmidhi, Tohfatul Ahwazi, Shukani, Shah Waliullah, and the contemporary Grand Mufti Ibn Baz) converge on this position, and the OIC Fiqh Academy and Shari'ah boards of Islamic banks approve the difference in cash/credit price as legitimate. Sarakhsi is quoted at length arguing selling on credit is 'an absolute feature of trade' and the primary means by which a Musharakah investor's profit goal is achieved; Udovitch's commentary that the cash/credit price difference 'fulfils, from the point of view of its economic function, the same role as interest' is noted but explicitly rejected by Ayub, who argues Islamic economics ties value to conversion of money into an asset (whose value can rise or fall over time), not to a rent charged on money itself. The critical, absolutely binding rule: the price, once mutually stipulated, cannot later be enhanced due to delay in payment -- doing so recreates the pre-Islamic Riba practice condemned in the Qur'an (2:275-276), where sellers who extended payment time in exchange for a price increase were rebuked ('Allah has permitted trading, and prohibited Riba'). If a seller offers two undetermined alternative prices tied to two different future payment dates without one being finally fixed at contract time, the sale is void for uncertainty.","examExplanation":"No price/profit-margin fixing by Shari'ah (demand/supply governs), except to prevent Ghaban-e-Fahish (exorbitant price disguised as market price -- gives buyer rescission right). Credit price CAN exceed cash price, IF: (a) only one final price is fixed at the time of contract (before the parties separate), and (b) that price is never later changed due to payment delay. What's prohibited: leaving price open/floating based on how late payment is (e.g. '$10 if paid in a month, $12 if paid in two, unresolved') -- void for uncertainty; and increasing an already-fixed price after default (this recreates pre-Islamic Riba, per Qur'an 2:275-276 background). Sarakhsi: credit sale is 'an absolute feature of trade.' Ayub explicitly rejects Udovitch's claim that the cash/credit price gap 'functions like interest.'","keyPoints":["Islam does not fix prices or profit margins by default -- demand and supply govern, subject to moral/ethical limits; government price-fixing is reserved for clear market abuse.","Ghaban-e-Fahish (excessive profiteering with deception -- charging an exorbitant price while implying it is the market price) is prohibited; the defrauded buyer may rescind.","A seller may validly quote a higher credit price than cash price, provided one definite price is fixed at the time of the bargain (before the parties separate) and the practice reflects genuine market/trade dealing.","The critical rule: once a price is mutually stipulated, it can never later be increased due to delay in payment -- this would recreate the pre-Islamic Riba practice the Qur'an condemns.","If a seller offers two undetermined prices tied to different future payment dates, with neither finally settled at contract time, the sale is void for price uncertainty.","Sarakhsi: selling on credit is 'an absolute feature of trade' and the principal means by which a merchant/investor achieves profit.","Ayub explicitly rejects Udovitch's claim that the cash/credit price difference functions economically the same as interest -- Islamic economics ties value to an asset's changing worth, not to a time-based rent on money itself.","A buyer who has taken possession of a credit-purchased asset may resell it even before completing payment (this is not selling what he does not own)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ghaban-e-Fahish","definition":"Excessive profiteering with deception -- a seller explicitly states or implies he's charging the market price while actually charging an exorbitant price, exploiting the buyer's ignorance."}],"conditions":["If a seller quotes both a cash and credit price, one of the two must be settled within the same contracting session, and the difference must reflect genuine market practice rather than Ghaban-e-Fahish.","A credit price, once mutually stipulated, must remain fixed and cannot be later increased due to payment delay."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Valid two-tier pricing","kind":"textbook","text":"A seller declares: 'The price will be $10 if you purchase it today and $11 tomorrow' -- valid, as the seller has the right to price according to the timing of sale, and the difference is in lieu of the item, not a reward for time per se, as long as one price is finally settled at the time of the deal."},{"title":"Void uncertain pricing","kind":"textbook","text":"A says to B: 'If you pay within a month, the price is $10; if after two months, $12,' and B agrees without settling on one of the two -- the sale is void because the price remains uncertain."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often think ANY higher credit price is disguised Riba; the chapter's detailed jurist discussion clarifies the difference is legitimate as long as one price is fixed at contract time and never changed thereafter -- what's actually Riba is increasing an already-agreed price because of a payment delay.","The Udovitch view (that cash/credit price differences function economically like interest) is presented and then explicitly rejected by the author -- students should not treat this as the book's own conclusion."],"distinctions":["Legitimate credit-price premium (fixed once, at contract time, never changed) vs Riba (price increased after default/delay)"],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' Mu'ajjal","Murabaha","Time value of money","Ghaban-e-Fahish"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.2","pages":[138,139,140,141,142]}}]},{"id":"t6.5.3","section":"6.5.3","title":"Cash and Credit Prices","pages":[139,142],"tier":"core","concepts":["time-value","murabaha"],"intuition":"Why may an Islamic bank charge more for a credit sale than a cash sale — and what exactly is forbidden?","simpleExplanation":"Most jurists allow a seller to quote a cash and a credit price, provided one price is settled before the parties separate. Even a single higher credit price is allowed by many (mostly Hanafi and Shafi‘i). What is forbidden is increasing the price after it is fixed because of late payment. A credit buyer owns the goods from day one and may resell them before paying in full.","academicExplanation":["Credit trade was widespread in medieval Islamic commerce. Most jurists allow two prices (cash and credit) if one is settled in the same meeting, the difference is normal market practice, the aim is trade and there is no Ghaban-e-Fahish. The Hadith “the person who makes two bargains in one sale, the lower is lawful for him or he is charging Riba” refers to not settling one price. Where only a higher credit price is declared, jurists differ; those disapproving fear an excess for delay; mostly Shafi‘i and Hanafi jurists allow it since the whole price is stipulated at the bargain — like $10 today and $11 tomorrow, the difference being in lieu of the item, not a reward for time. Tirmidhi, Tohfatul Ahwazi, Shukani (the ‘Illah of prohibition is non-fixity of price), Shah Waliullah and Shaikh Ibn Baz permit a settled credit price.","Sarakhsi: selling on credit is an absolute feature of trade; a thing is sold on credit for more than for cash. Udovitch argued the difference fulfils the economic role of interest; the author says he overstates it: Islam converts money into assets whose value may rise or fall; $1000 today is $1000 tomorrow, but a $1000 asset may be worth more or less. Verse 2:275 records the usurers’ objection that raising a price at the outset equals raising it at maturity; Allah permitted trade and forbade Riba (Ibn Abi Hatim, Mujahid via Sayyuti, Qatadah via Tabari).","Conclusion: a credit price above the spot price is acceptable (approved by the OIC Fiqh Academy and all Islamic banks’ Shari’ah boards); prohibited is enhancing the stipulated price for delay, because the sold goods belong to the buyer and the price becomes a debt. Absolute certainty of price is necessary: “$10 within a month, $12 after two months” without choosing is void. A Murabaha buyer (e.g. a car payable over five years) owns it from day one and can sell it after a year to someone who takes over the instalments — this is not selling what he does not own."],"examExplanation":"Credit price > cash price allowed if one price settled before separation (OIC Fiqh Academy; Shari’ah boards). Prohibited: increasing agreed price for delay (price = debt). Udovitch’s ‘same role as interest’ overstated. Credit buyer owns from day one and may resell.","keyPoints":["Shukani: the ‘Illah of ‘two sales in one’ is non-fixity of price.","Difference in price is in lieu of the item, not time per se.","Credit buyer may resell before full payment."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Reselling a Murabaha car","kind":"textbook","text":"Client C buys a car on five-year Murabaha. After a year he sells it to Y, who takes over the instalments — valid, because C has owned the car from day one (p. 142)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t4.6","t9.6.1","t3.2.1.b"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A client bought a car on five-year Murabaha. After one year he sells it to Y, who takes over the instalments. This is:","options":["Sale of what he does not own — void","Valid — he owns the car from day one","Bai‘ al ‘Inah","Riba"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 142."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"debate":[{"issue":"Is the cash–credit price difference just interest in disguise?","criticism":"Udovitch: the difference does not formally constitute interest but fulfils the same economic role — a return for risk and absence of capital.","response":"The author: this overstates the case. Islam values money only through assets; the price difference arises from selling a commodity whose value may change, it creates no rent on capital and, once fixed, cannot grow with time.","alternative":"Some jurists disapprove of a single higher credit price if the buyer perceives an excess for delay.","takeaway":"A fixed credit price agreed at the bargain is trade; any later increase for delay is Riba."},{"issue":"Is a credit mark-up the same as interest?","criticism":"Udovitch argues the cash–credit price difference fulfils the same economic role as interest — a return for risk and for the absence of capital.","response":"The author says Udovitch overstates the case: Islamic finance converts money into an asset and values the asset; it concedes time value in pricing credit sales but does not make capital earn rent, so no rentier class results. Once fixed, the price becomes a debt that cannot grow.","alternative":"Jurists (Shukani, Shah Waliullah, Ibn Baz) allow a higher credit price if one price is settled before the parties separate.","takeaway":"A credit price is permissible; increasing it after the sale is Riba."}],"calc":{"type":"credit-price","note":"Practice calculator: cash price, agreed mark-up and instalments; shows that late payment cannot change the debt."}},{"id":"t6.6","section":"6.6","title":"Riba Involvement in Sales","pages":[142,143],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["riba-fadl","bai-sarf"],"intuition":"Where can Riba creep into a sale? In exchanges of monetary items and same-genus goods.","simpleExplanation":"Exchanging Athman (monetary items) of the same kind requires mutual spot delivery and equality. Currency sales must be instant; a normal settlement period is allowed if not made a condition. A Shari’ah-compliant credit card may be used to buy gold, since an unintentional delay of up to 72 hours is not a problem (OIC Fiqh Academy, Al Baraka).","academicExplanation":["Mutual exchange of ‘Ayan (commodities of intrinsic value) follows different rules from exchange of Athman. When an article of the Thaman kind is exchanged for the same kind, mutual delivery and equal weight are required (six-commodities Hadith). Currency sales must be instant, not deferred; a normal settlement time is allowed if it is not a condition of exchange. The OIC Fiqh Academy and Al Baraka’s Shari’ah committee allow an otherwise compliant credit card to buy gold and silver, as an unintentional delay of up to 72 hours does not create a problem."],"examExplanation":"Athman of same kind: equal + spot. Currency sales instant; normal settlement time allowed if not a condition; credit card for gold OK (≤72-hour unintentional delay).","keyPoints":["Normal settlement time must not be a condition of the exchange.","Normal settlement time in currency deals is allowed provided the delay is not made a condition of the exchange."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Credit cards for gold","kind":"textbook","text":"The OIC Fiqh Academy and Al Baraka’s Shari’ah committee allow an otherwise compliant credit card to buy gold and silver, since an unintentional settlement delay of up to 72 hours is tolerated (p. 143)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.1.d","t4.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why is using a compliant credit card to buy gold considered acceptable by the bodies cited?","options":["Gold is not Thaman","An unintentional delay of up to 72 hours in payment does not create a problem","Credit cards charge interest","It is Salam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 143."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch6-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch6-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"6.6","sectionTitle":"Riba Involvement in Sales","title":"Riba Al-Fadl in Sales and Currency Exchange Rules","covers":["t6.6"],"overview":"Sales can involve Riba Al-Fadl when homogeneous Ribawi commodities (like gold-for-gold or wheat-for-wheat) are exchanged unequally or with delay; currency exchange must be instant, not deferred.","simpleExplanation":"When you swap identical kinds of certain goods -- like gold for gold, or wheat for wheat -- Islamic law says the amounts must be exactly equal and the exchange must happen on the spot, or it becomes interest. If the two things are genuinely different (like gold for wheat), you can trade any quantities you like, but it still has to happen hand-to-hand if both are 'money-like' items such as currencies.","academicExplanation":"Sales contracts can involve Riba Al-Fadl, governed by rules distinguishing the exchange of 'Ayan (commodities of material/intrinsic value) from Athman (items with monetary value/price function). When articles of the Thaman (price) kind are exchanged for an article of the same kind, the law requires mutual (hand-to-hand) delivery and exact equality in weight/measure -- based on the Hadith: 'Gold for gold, silver for silver, wheat for wheat, barley for barley, dates for dates, salt for salt -- like for like, equal for equal, and hand to hand; if the commodities differ, sell as you wish, provided the exchange is hand to hand.' Currency and foreign exchange dealings, being routine bank activities, must be exchanged instantly, not on a deferred basis; a normal settlement-processing delay is tolerated by scholars provided it's not made a condition of the exchange itself (e.g. OIC Fiqh Academy and Al Baraka Bank's Shari'ah advisory allow up to 72 hours' unintentional delay for Shari'ah-compliant credit card purchases of gold/silver).","examExplanation":"Riba Al-Fadl rule: same-genus Ribawi items (gold-gold, wheat-wheat, etc.) must be exchanged equal-for-equal AND hand-to-hand (spot); different-genus items can be exchanged in any quantity but must still be hand-to-hand if both are Athman (monetary-type items, e.g. currencies). A brief unintentional settlement delay (e.g. up to 72 hours, per specific rulings) is tolerated if not made a contractual condition.","keyPoints":["'Ayan (intrinsic-value commodities) and Athman (monetary-value items) are governed by different exchange rules.","Same-genus Ribawi items (gold-gold, silver-silver, wheat-wheat, barley-barley, dates-dates, salt-salt) must be exchanged equal-for-equal and hand-to-hand (spot) -- delay or inequality creates Riba Al-Fadl.","Different-genus items may be exchanged in any quantity, but the exchange must still be hand-to-hand if the items are of the Thaman (price/monetary) kind.","Currency and forex dealings must be instant/spot, not deferred; a normal, unintentional settlement delay (not made a contractual condition) is tolerated by scholars, e.g. up to 72 hours for certain credit-card gold/silver purchases per specific rulings."],"definitions":[{"term":"'Ayan","definition":"Commodities of material/intrinsic value in themselves (as opposed to Athman)."},{"term":"Athman","definition":"Items having monetary value/price function -- the medium of exchange used for payment of prices and liabilities."}],"conditions":["Exchange of same-genus Ribawi commodities requires exact equality in weight/measure and hand-to-hand (spot) delivery.","Currency/monetary exchanges must be instant, not deferred, though a brief unintentional settlement delay not made a condition of the exchange is tolerated."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think all Bai' involving different goods must be spot; the rule is that hand-to-hand delivery is required for same-genus Ribawi exchanges and for exchanges where both sides are Athman (monetary items) -- goods of different genus and nature (e.g. wheat for cash on credit) can involve delay as an ordinary credit sale."],"distinctions":["Riba Al-Fadl (excess in same-genus exchange) vs Riba Al-Nasiah (excess/delay in loan/debt transactions) -- covered together with Chapter 3/4 material"],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al-Sarf","Ribawi goods","Riba Al-Fadl","Riba Al-Nasiah"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.6","pages":[142,143]}}]},{"id":"t6.7","section":"6.7","title":"Gharar — A Cause of Prohibition of Sales","pages":[143,144],"tier":"core","concepts":["gharar"],"intuition":"Which specific sales are prohibited because of Gharar — and which modern ones?","simpleExplanation":"The Prophet prohibited sales like Bai‘ al-Ma‘dum (nonexistent), Mulamasah (by touching), Munabadhah (throwing garments without examining) and Bai‘ al-Hasat (pebble). Imam Malik allowed selling bales by catalogue (Barnamaj). Today, trademarked or precisely specified goods may involve no Gharar. Book-out contracts — buying and selling without possession, settling only differences — are prohibited.","academicExplanation":["Business risk is not Gharar; the requirement is that goods be defined, determined, deliverable and known, with quality and quantity stipulated and no Jahl. Prohibited: Bai‘ al-Ma‘dum, Mulamasah (touching a garment without unfolding it), Munabadhah (throwing garments to each other without examination), Bai‘ al-Hasat. Imam Malik disallowed selling a Persian shawl in its cover, but allowed wholesale bales sold by an accompanying catalogue (Barnamaj), since that is established practice and not intended as a risk. Today goods under trademarks or with minute specifications may involve no Gharar; many jurists soften conditions for nonedibles, and religious boards allow banks to agree to provide nonedible goods after buying them from the market. A common modern Gharar-based transaction is the book-out contract — buying and selling without possession, settling the price difference — in commodities, stocks and FX; Shari’ah boards prohibit it."],"examExplanation":"Gharar sales: Ma‘dum, Mulamasah, Munabadhah, Hasat. Malik allowed Barnamaj (catalogue) sales. Trademarks/specifications reduce Gharar. Book-out contracts (difference settlement) prohibited.","keyPoints":["Barnamaj: bale sale by catalogue is allowed.","Book-out contracts are prohibited."],"definitions":[{"term":"Mulamasah","definition":"Sale by touching a garment without unfolding or ascertaining it."},{"term":"Munabadhah","definition":"Throwing garments to each other in exchange without either examining them."},{"term":"Barnamaj","definition":"Catalogue/list of contents by which wholesale bales were sold unopened — allowed by Imam Malik."},{"term":"Book-out contract","definition":"Buying an asset and selling it without taking possession, settling only the price difference."}],"examples":[{"title":"Folded shawl vs catalogued bales","kind":"textbook","text":"Imam Malik forbade selling a Persian shawl in its cover unseen, but allowed selling bales by a catalogue (Barnamaj), since wholesale trade would otherwise be impossible and the practice involves no intended risk (pp. 143–144)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"All business risk is Gharar.","right":"Business risk is a valid requirement for return; Gharar is uncertainty about the subject matter, price or rights (p. 143)."}],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.2","t8.8.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which sale did Imam Malik permit despite goods not being unfolded?","options":["A Persian shawl in its cover","Wholesale bales sold by catalogue (Barnamaj)","Mulamasah","Munabadhah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 144."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch6-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch6-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"6.7","sectionTitle":"Gharar -- A Cause of Prohibition of Sales","title":"Gharar in Sales: Prohibited Forms and Modern Applications","covers":["t6.7"],"overview":"Gharar (uncertainty/hazard about a sale's subject matter, price, or outcome) invalidates sales; classical examples include Mulamasah, Munabadhah, and Bai' al-Hasat, while modern book-out/derivative-style contracts are analogous prohibited forms.","simpleExplanation":"You can't sell something if either side doesn't really know what they're getting or what it's worth -- like selling a wrapped item without letting the buyer check it, or a fish still swimming in the water. Business risk itself is fine and even necessary for earning a legitimate profit; what's banned is a deal where the basic facts are unknown or the outcome depends on pure chance.","academicExplanation":"Gharar means hazard, chance, stake or risk; legally, it is the sale of a thing not present at hand, or whose consequence/outcome is unknown, or where one doesn't know if it will materialize (e.g. fish in water, a bird in the air). Jurists derive the general principle that a sale must not be doubtful/uncertain regarding rights and obligations, else it amounts to deceiving the other party -- the object, price, and terms must be precisely determined, known and clear. Business risk itself is not Gharar; risk-bearing is in fact a valid requirement for legitimately earning a return in exchange -- what's prohibited is uncertainty about the very existence, definition, or deliverability of the goods or price (this includes Jahl, ignorance). The Prophet prohibited a cluster of Gharar-laden sale forms: Bai' al-Ma'dum (sale of the nonexistent), Bai' al-Mulamasah (touching a wrapped item without unfolding/inspecting it as the basis of sale), Bai' al-Munabadhah (exchanging thrown garments without either examining them), and Bai' al-Hasat (sale determined by the fall of a pebble/stone). Imam Malik permits, however, the well-established wholesale practice of selling bales per an accompanying descriptive catalogue (Barnamaj) without unfolding them, distinguishing this from Mulamasah since it is not intended as risk and is a known, accepted market practice. In the modern age, minute specification and trademarking reduce Gharar in many nonedible goods, and religious boards permit banks to agree to provide such goods after purchasing them from the market. A prominent contemporary Gharar-based transaction is the 'book-out' contract -- buying an asset and selling it without taking possession, settling only the price difference -- common in commodities, stock, and forex markets (a major share of global forex trading is book-out, involving speculation and excessive risk-taking with no real exchange, only paper entries); Shari'ah committees and boards have declared such transactions prohibited.","examExplanation":"Gharar = uncertainty about subject matter, price, or outcome of a sale; distinguished from ordinary business risk (which IS allowed and even necessary for legitimate profit). Prohibited classical Gharar forms: Bai' al-Ma'dum (nonexistent item), Mulamasah (touch-only, no unfolding/inspection), Munabadhah (thrown-garment exchange, no examination), Bai' al-Hasat (pebble-fall determines the sale). Exception: sale of bales per a descriptive catalogue (Barnamaj) without unfolding is valid -- it's accepted market practice, not risk-taking. Modern analog: book-out contracts (buy+sell without possession, only price difference settled) -- prohibited, common in commodity/stock/forex speculation.","keyPoints":["Gharar = hazard/uncertainty about the existence, definition, deliverability, or outcome of the subject matter or price of a sale.","Business risk itself is not Gharar and is in fact necessary for legitimately earning a return in exchange transactions.","Prohibited classical Gharar forms: Bai' al-Ma'dum (nonexistent item), Bai' al-Mulamasah (touch without unfolding), Bai' al-Munabadhah (thrown-garment exchange, unexamined), Bai' al-Hasat (sale by chance of a pebble's fall).","Imam Malik permitted sale of bales per a descriptive catalogue (Barnamaj) without unfolding, as an accepted, non-risky wholesale practice distinct from Mulamasah.","Modern trademarking and precise specification reduce Gharar concerns for many nonedible goods, allowing bank purchase-and-supply arrangements.","Book-out contracts (buy-and-sell an asset without possession, settling only the price difference) are a modern Gharar-based transaction, prohibited by Shari'ah boards; a large share of global forex trading is book-out and involves excessive speculation."],"definitions":[{"term":"Gharar","definition":"Hazard, chance, stake or risk; legally, the sale of a thing not present at hand, or whose consequence/outcome is unknown, involving excessive uncertainty about the subject matter or its counter-value."},{"term":"Mulamasah","definition":"A sale where a person touches or feels a wrapped garment without unfolding or examining it, as the basis for the sale -- prohibited for Gharar."},{"term":"Munabadhah","definition":"A sale where two parties throw garments to each other in exchange, without either examining them -- prohibited for Gharar."}],"conditions":["The object of sale must be defined, determined, deliverable, and clearly known to both parties in quality and quantity.","Quality and quantity must be stipulated, and the actual state of the goods must be known to the parties (avoiding Jahl/ignorance)."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Barnamaj (catalogue) sale of bales","kind":"textbook","text":"Imam Malik permitted selling whole bales of goods based on an accompanying descriptive catalogue (Barnamaj), without unfolding them -- distinguished from Mulamasah because it's an accepted, non-risky wholesale market practice, not intended as a gamble."},{"title":"Book-out contracts in forex/commodities","kind":"textbook","text":"A person buys an asset and sells it again without taking possession, only netting the price difference -- common in commodities, stocks, and especially forex markets; declared prohibited by Shari'ah committees as it involves speculation and no real exchange of ownership."}],"commonConfusions":["Business risk and Gharar are often conflated; the text is explicit that not all risk is Gharar -- ordinary business risk (e.g. price fluctuation, storage risk) is a valid and necessary basis for earning legitimate profit, while Gharar specifically concerns uncertainty about the very existence, definition or deliverability of the subject matter/price."],"distinctions":["Ordinary business risk (permissible, basis for profit) vs Gharar (excessive uncertainty, prohibited)","Barnamaj/catalogue sale (valid, accepted market practice) vs Mulamasah (invalid, no real inspection basis)"],"relatedConcepts":["Salam","Istisna'a","Jahl","Book-out/derivatives"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.7","pages":[143,144]}}]},{"id":"t6.8","section":"6.8","title":"Conditional Sales and “Two Bargains in One Sale”","pages":[144,145],"tier":"core","concepts":["two-contracts","tawarruq"],"intuition":"Why is ‘I’ll buy your goods if you lend me money’ prohibited?","simpleExplanation":"Sales conditional on uncertain events, or bundled with gifts, marriage, loans or Shirkah, are prohibited. Bai‘ wal Salaf (sale combined with a loan) is unlawful because it can hide compensation for the loan. Ibn Taymiyah objects mainly to combining onerous and gratuitous contracts. Contracts can be combined informally, without conditioning one on another — e.g. Tawarruq.","academicExplanation":["Shari’ah disapproves of sales conditional on matters that may or may not happen. Fiqh prohibits Shartaan fi Ba‘ien and Bai‘ wal Shart involving lack of clarity and unjustifiable benefit (e.g. “I will sell you this house if a third person sells me his”). Conditions of gift, marriage, Qard or Shirkah within a sale render it prohibited. Hanafis consider conditional sales a type of gambling (Ibn Abideen); Ibn Taymiyah and Ibnul Qayyim allow certain suspended sales without Gharar or Riba, rejecting only conditions contradicting clear texts or consensus or the contract’s object. Ibn Hazm lists seven enforceable conditions, including Rihn and a stipulated credit period. The Prophet: “unlawful are a sale and a loan (Bai‘ wal Salaf), or two stipulations in a sale, or a sale of what you do not have.” Malik: “I’ll buy your goods for such if you lend me such” is not permitted unless the loan condition is abandoned; Imam Ahmad: giving a loan then selling something at above market. Combining conditional contracts confuses rights and opens the door to Riba and Gharar. Ibn Taymiyah (followed by modern scholars) objects only to combining onerous and gratuitous contracts. Contracts may be combined informally, e.g. Tawarruq (buy on credit, sell separately for cash to another party), declared permissible by most scholars. A price must not be bundled with a gift — reduce the price instead."],"examExplanation":"Prohibited: sale conditional on uncertain event; sale bundled with gift/marriage/Qard/Shirkah; Bai‘ wal Salaf; two stipulations in a sale. Ibn Taymiyah: only onerous + gratuitous combination objectionable. Informal combination (Tawarruq) permitted by most.","keyPoints":["Bai‘ wal Salaf can conceal compensation for a loan.","Offer a price reduction instead of a ‘gift’ with the price.","Ibn Hazm lists seven enforceable conditions, including a pledge (Rihn) and a stipulated credit period."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai‘ wal Salaf","definition":"Combining a sale and a loan in one transaction (e.g. “I will buy your goods if you lend me money”) — unlawful."}],"examples":[{"title":"Gift bundled with price","kind":"textbook","text":"“Sell me this and I will give you a gift in addition to the price” involves Gharar and Jahl; the seller should instead reduce the price so the counter value is exact (p. 145)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t5.5.5","t13.3","t5.9"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why does Ibn Taymiyah object to combining a sale with a loan?","options":["Because loans are haram","Because parties can hide an illegal compensation for the loan","Because sales need witnesses","He does not object"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 145."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch6-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch6-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"6.8","sectionTitle":"Conditional Sales and \"Two Bargains in One Sale\"","title":"Conditional Sales, Two Stipulations, and Combining Sale with Loan","covers":["t6.8"],"overview":"The Shari'ah generally disapproves sales conditional on uncertain future events or combined with unrelated obligations (like loans or gifts), viewing such bundling as a gateway to Riba and Gharar.","simpleExplanation":"You can't make a sale depend on some unrelated 'if' (like 'I'll sell you my house if someone else sells me theirs') because that's basically gambling on an outcome. You also can't combine a sale with a loan in a way that ties the price to the favor of lending money, because that's a sneaky way of charging interest. A few scholars are more flexible about conditions that don't clash with clear Islamic law, but combining sale and loan specifically is treated very strictly because it's an easy way to disguise interest as a business deal.","academicExplanation":"The Shari'ah disapproves sales conditional on chance-based future events. Fiqh literature identifies two related prohibitions: Shartaan fi Bai'ein (a sale with two stipulations) and Bai' wal Shart (lacking clarity, giving unjustified benefit to one party) -- e.g. 'I will sell you this house if a third person sells me his house,' which introduces Gharar regarding the timing/finalization of the contract. Conditions tying a gift, marriage, Qard, or Shirkah into a sale contract render it prohibited. Hanafi jurists (e.g. Ibn Abideen) treat such conditional/future-contingent ownership-transfer contracts as a form of gambling. Ibn Taymiyah and Ibn al-Qayyim, however, are more liberal, permitting certain suspended sales that don't involve genuine Gharar or Riba -- Ibn Taymiyah rejects only stipulations contradicting explicit Qur'an/Sunnah/Ijma'a text or the very object of the contract; Ibn Hazm lists seven types of enforceable conditions (e.g. Rihn in Bai', stipulated delayed payment in credit sale, goods' traits/features). The Prophet's Hadith explicitly prohibits combining a sale and a loan (Bai' wal Salaf) or two stipulations in a sale, or selling what one doesn't have. Imam Malik defines Bai' wal Salaf as: 'I shall purchase your goods for such and such if you lend me such and such' -- impermissible if agreed as bundled, but permissible if the loan-stipulation party abandons that condition. Shah Waliullah explains this as co-mingling a loan with a sale, generating Jahl/hazard. Imam Ahmad reads it as giving a loan and then selling something to the borrower above market price. Ibn Taymiyah, the most liberal cited voice, objects specifically to combining onerous (compensatory) and gratuitous contracts, like sale and Qard, since such bundling easily hides illegal loan-compensation. An accepted informal workaround is Tawarruq (buying something on credit, then immediately reselling it for cash in a separate, non-legally-conditioned transaction with a different party) -- most scholars permit it since such sequential behavior, if not legally conditioned, cannot be regulated by law, only by moral ruling. A related prohibited bundling: tying a sale to a gift with unquantified value ('sell me this; I'll give you that much extra as a gift') -- this involves Gharar/Jahl, and the correct approach is simply to lower the price to fix an exact counter-value.","examExplanation":"Prohibited: conditional/contingent sale on an unrelated future event ('I'll sell if X happens' -- Gharar); Bai' wal Salaf (sale conditioned on a loan being extended -- Riba risk, since parties can hide illegal loan compensation); two unstipulated bargains in one sale without a final price fixed. Ibn Taymiyah's liberal stance: only reject stipulations contradicting explicit Nass/Ijma'a or the contract's core object -- otherwise permit. Ibn Hazm lists 7 enforceable condition types (Rihn, delayed-payment stipulation, traits of goods, etc.). Tawarruq (credit purchase then immediate, separately-negotiated cash resale, not legally bundled) is permitted by most scholars as a workaround, though morally discouraged in spirit.","keyPoints":["Shartaan fi Bai'ein (two stipulations in a sale) and Bai' wal Shart (an unclear, one-sided-benefit condition) are prohibited forms of conditional sale.","A sale made contingent on an unrelated uncertain future event (e.g. 'I'll sell if X sells me his house') is treated by Hanafi jurists as a form of gambling.","Ibn Taymiyah and Ibn al-Qayyim take a more liberal stance, permitting suspended sales/conditions that don't involve genuine Gharar or Riba, rejecting only conditions that contradict explicit Nass, Ijma'a, or the contract's core purpose.","The Prophet explicitly prohibited Bai' wal Salaf (combining sale and loan in one bargain) and 'two stipulations in a sale.'","Combining onerous (compensatory, e.g. sale) and gratuitous (e.g. Qard) contracts is especially risky because it can hide illegal compensation for a loan -- Ibn Taymiyah singles this combination out as most objectionable.","Tawarruq (credit purchase, then a separate, non-legally-conditioned cash resale to a third party) is permitted by most scholars as an informal, non-bundled workaround for obtaining liquidity.","Tying an unquantified gift to a sale price ('I'll give you extra as a gift') is prohibited for Gharar/Jahl; the correct approach is to simply lower the price."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai' wal Salaf","definition":"A conditional contract combining selling and lending, e.g. one party saying 'I purchase your goods for such and such if you lend me such and such' -- invalid when bundled as a condition."},{"term":"Tawarruq","definition":"A transaction where a person buys something on credit and then, in a separate transaction with a different party, immediately sells it for cash to obtain liquidity."}],"conditions":["A sale must not be made contingent on an unrelated uncertain future event.","A sale must not be legally conditioned on the other party extending a loan or gift.","For Tawarruq to be acceptable, the purchase and resale must be genuinely separate transactions, not legally bundled or conditioned on each other."],"principles":["Ibn Taymiyah's approach: only reject stipulations that contradict explicit Qur'an/Sunnah/Ijma'a text or the very object of the contract."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Contingent house sale (Gharar)","kind":"textbook","text":"A says to B: 'I will sell you this house if any third person sells me his house.' This is prohibited for Gharar regarding the timing, condition, and finalization of the contract."},{"title":"Bai' wal Salaf","kind":"textbook","text":"One person says to another: 'I shall purchase your goods for such and such if you lend me such and such.' If agreed as bundled, this is not permitted; if the loan-stipulating party drops that condition, the sale itself is permitted."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume all conditional sales are banned outright; the text shows real scholarly divergence -- Hanafi jurists (Ibn Abideen) treat conditional/future-contingent transfers as gambling, while Ibn Taymiyah and Ibn al-Qayyim permit many conditions as long as they don't contradict explicit text or the contract's core purpose.","Tawarruq is often mistaken for being identical to prohibited Bai' al-'Inah; the key distinguishing feature (elaborated further in Section 6.11) is that Tawarruq's purchase and resale are with different, unrelated parties and not legally conditioned on each other."],"distinctions":["Sale contingent on an unrelated event (prohibited -- Gharar/gambling-like) vs a condition that supports/doesn't contradict the contract's core purpose (may be valid per Ibn Taymiyah)","Bai' wal Salaf (sale legally bundled with a loan -- prohibited) vs Tawarruq (sale and resale genuinely separate, not legally conditioned -- permitted by most scholars)"],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al-'Inah","Qard","T'aliq","Gharar"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.8","pages":[144,145,146]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Formally combined contracts","b":"Informally combined contracts","text":"Contracts legally conditioned on each other confuse rights and open the door to Riba; separate transactions not conditioned on each other — like Tawarruq — are accepted by most scholars (p. 145)."}]},{"id":"t6.9","section":"6.9","title":"Bai‘ al ‘Arbun (Downpayment Sale)","pages":[145,146],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["arbun"],"intuition":"Can a seller keep a buyer’s downpayment if the buyer walks away?","simpleExplanation":"‘Arbun: a downpayment that becomes part of the price if the buyer proceeds and is forfeited if not. Most traditional jurists prohibited it (Gharar); Hanbalis allow it. The author concludes forfeiture may not be permissible where unforeseen events cause injustice, but customary ‘Arbun with a specified time limit is allowed (OIC Fiqh Council, AAOIFI).","academicExplanation":["Two traditions: one forbidding ‘Arbun (Malik), one permitting it (Zaid ibn Aslam). Prohibited by Hanafis, Malikis, Shafi‘is, Zaidis and Abul Khattab (Hanbali); approved by Imam Ahmad on the authority of Umar and some Tabi‘een. Ibn Rushd: the majority forbid it for Gharar, risk-taking and taking money without consideration. Ibn Qudama compares it to paying a seller to rescind, or paying a seller not to sell to others and later deducting that payment from the price — hinting that if the final sale is not concluded, the advance would be unearned and returnable. The author: where absolute Gharar or injustice to a buyer who cannot complete due to unforeseen events exists, confiscation may not be permissible; as customary practice with free consent, it is permissible on the basis of ‘Urf. The OIC Islamic Fiqh Council and AAOIFI allow customary downpayment sale with a specified time limit."],"examExplanation":"‘Arbun: downpayment, part of price or forfeited. Majority (Hanafi, Maliki, Shafi‘i) prohibit; Hanbalis allow. OIC Fiqh Council and AAOIFI allow with a specified time limit; author: not where unforeseen events make forfeiture unjust.","keyPoints":["Time limit must be specified."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Ibn Qudama’s analogy","kind":"textbook","text":"He likens ‘Arbun to paying a seller to rescind a sale, or to paying a seller not to sell to anyone else and later deducting that sum from the price (p. 146)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t5.6.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"On what condition do the OIC Fiqh Council and AAOIFI allow downpayment (‘Arbun) sale?","options":["No condition","That a time limit is specified","Only for real estate","Only if refunded"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 146."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch6-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch6-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"6.9","sectionTitle":"Bai' al-'Arbun (Downpayment Sale)","title":"Bai' al-'Arbun: Contested Legality of Forfeitable Downpayment Sale","covers":["t6.9"],"overview":"Bai' al-'Arbun -- a sale where the buyer's downpayment becomes part of the price if he completes the purchase, or is forfeited if he doesn't -- is disputed among jurists, generally disapproved by the majority but accepted by Hanbalis and, under conditions, by the OIC Fiqh Council and AAOIFI.","simpleExplanation":"'Arbun is like a non-refundable deposit: if you go through with the purchase, it counts toward the price; if you back out, the seller keeps it. Most classical scholars disliked this because it lets someone take money for nothing if the deal falls through, which feels like unearned gain and uncertainty. But the Hanbali school allowed it, and modern Islamic finance bodies now permit a customary version of it as long as there's a clear time limit.","academicExplanation":"Bai' al-'Arbun is a sale of downpayment where, if the buyer takes the commodity, the downpayment becomes part of the price; if he doesn't purchase, the advance is forfeited. Two conflicting Hadith traditions exist: one (via Imam Malik) has the Prophet forbidding 'Arbun sale; another (via Zaid ibn Aslam) has the Prophet permitting it when asked. The majority of traditional jurists (Hanafis, Malikis, Shafi'es, Zaidi Shiites, and even some Hanbalis like Abul Khattab) prohibit it for Gharar, risk-taking, and taking money without consideration; only the Hanbali school (via Imam Ahmad, citing precedent from 'Umar and several Tabi'een) approves it. Ibn Qudama (a Hanbali jurist) justifies 'Arbun by analogy to two related contracts: (1) a buyer paying a seller to rescind a sale, and (2) a potential buyer paying a potential seller to hold goods exclusively for him pending a later, unconditioned final sale (with the initial payment deducted from the price) -- though Ibn Qudama notes that in this second analogy, if the final sale doesn't conclude, the advance is unearned gain and must be returned on demand, suggesting a nuanced, not absolute, forfeiture right. Ayub's synthesis: where absolute Gharar or injustice to the buyer is involved (e.g. an unforeseen event prevents completing an otherwise-committed purchase), downpayment confiscation may not be permissible; but to the extent the practice is customary ('Urf) with free consent and unforeseen events accounted for, it is permissible. The OIC Islamic Fiqh Council and AAOIFI have both allowed customary 'Arbun sale, on condition that a time limit is specified.","examExplanation":"Bai' al-'Arbun = forfeitable downpayment sale (becomes part of price if completed, forfeited if not). Majority view (Hanafi, Maliki, Shafi'e, Zaidi Shia, and some Hanbalis): prohibited for Gharar/unearned gain. Hanbali view (via Imam Ahmad): permitted, justified by analogy to paying for exclusive holding rights or paying to rescind a sale. Contemporary resolution (OIC Fiqh Council, AAOIFI): customary 'Arbun sale is allowed, conditioned on specifying a time limit. Nuance: full forfeiture may not be just where an unforeseen event (not the buyer's fault) prevents completion -- some jurists (e.g. via Ibn Qudama's second analogy) suggest the advance should be returned if the sale never concludes.","keyPoints":["Bai' al-'Arbun: downpayment becomes part of the price if the buyer completes the purchase; forfeited to the seller if he does not.","Two conflicting Hadith exist on 'Arbun -- one prohibiting, one (from Zaid ibn Aslam) permitting it.","Majority of traditional schools (Hanafi, Maliki, Shafi'e, Zaidi Shia, and even most Hanbalis) prohibit it, citing Gharar and unearned gain.","Only the mainstream Hanbali position (via Imam Ahmad) approves Bai' al-'Arbun as a valid, independent contract.","Ibn Qudama justifies 'Arbun by analogy to paying for a seller's exclusivity commitment or paying to rescind a sale.","Contemporary bodies (OIC Islamic Fiqh Council, AAOIFI) permit customary 'Arbun sale on condition that a specified time limit is set.","Full forfeiture may be inappropriate where an unforeseen event (beyond the buyer's control) prevents completing the purchase -- some analogy suggests the advance should then be returned."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai' al-'Arbun","definition":"A sale of downpayment with the condition that if the buyer takes the commodity, the downpayment becomes part of the selling price; if he doesn't purchase, the advance money is forfeited."}],"conditions":["For contemporary permissibility (OIC Fiqh Council, AAOIFI): a time limit for the buyer's decision must be specified."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume 'Arbun and Hamish Jiddiyah (Chapter 5) are the same device; 'Arbun is specifically a downpayment taken as part of the sale price after the sale agreement is executed and can (per Hanbali view) be wholly forfeited, whereas Hamish Jiddiyah is pre-execution earnest money limited to actual-loss recovery."],"distinctions":["Majority prohibition (Gharar/unearned gain) vs Hanbali permission (via analogy to exclusivity/rescission payments)","'Arbun (full forfeiture possible per Hanbali view) vs Hamish Jiddiyah (capped at actual loss, per Chapter 5)"],"relatedConcepts":["Hamish Jiddiyah","Wa'dah","Gharar"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.9","pages":[145,146]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Majority of classical jurists","b":"Hanbalis (and modern bodies)","text":"Hanafis, Malikis and Shafi‘is prohibit ‘Arbun for Gharar and taking money without consideration; Imam Ahmad allowed it. The OIC Fiqh Council and AAOIFI allow customary ‘Arbun with a specified time limit (pp. 145–146)."}]},{"id":"t6.10","section":"6.10","title":"Bai‘ al Dayn (Sale of Debt)","pages":[146,147],"tier":"core","concepts":["bai-dayn","hawalah"],"intuition":"Can an Islamic bank discount an export bill? No — but it can trade the goods itself or take an assignment.","simpleExplanation":"A debt (bill of exchange, export bill, promissory note) cannot be sold at a price other than face value; even at face value only with recourse, as in Hawalah. ‘Al Kali bil Kali’ (debt for debt) is prohibited by consensus. Murabaha rollover is its modern form and is prohibited. Asset-backed Sukuk and lease-based instruments can be traded.","academicExplanation":["Credit documents from credit sales represent debt that cannot be sold due to Gharar and/or Riba. A trader holding a bill or promissory note cannot sell it to an Islamic bank as to a conventional bank; instead the bank can buy the commodity from the producer and sell it on credit. The OIC Fiqh Academy and scholars consider sale of debt documents at other than nominal value incompatible; even at face value it is allowed only with recourse to the original debtor, as in Hawalah.","“Al Kali bil Kali” (Bai‘ al-Dayn bid-Dayn): exchange of two delayed counter values — e.g. a buyer unable to pay says “sell it to me on credit for a further period for something additional” — prohibited by the Prophet with near-universal consensus. Its modern form is Murabaha rollover on default, prohibited by all Shari’ah boards; any return is not legitimate income. In the early 1980s Pakistani banks purchased trade bills as Murabaha; the Council of Islamic Ideology and Shari’ah Courts disapproved.","Sale of debt is prohibited, but assignment (Hawalah) is permitted because of recourse. Certificates/Sukuk supported by assets become objects of value tradable in primary and secondary markets if returns come from the asset’s cash flow. Semi-debt instruments like leases can be traded, representing sale of leased assets at negotiated prices."],"examExplanation":"No sale of debt except at face value with recourse (Hawalah). Al Kali bil Kali prohibited by consensus; Murabaha rollover prohibited. Bill discounting not allowed (Pakistan 1980s disapproval). Asset-backed Sukuk and lease instruments tradable.","keyPoints":["Bank alternative to bill discounting: trade the goods itself.","Murabaha rollover = modern Al Kali bil Kali.","Asset-backed certificates are tradable.","Trade bills bought as “Murabaha” in Pakistan in the early 1980s were rejected by the CII and Shari’ah courts.","Asset-backed Sukuk and lease-based instruments can be traded, because they represent assets."],"definitions":[{"term":"Al Kali bil Kali / Bai‘ al-Dayn bid-Dayn","definition":"Exchange of a delayed counter value for another delayed counter value (debt for debt) — prohibited by consensus."}],"examples":[{"title":"Murabaha rollover","kind":"textbook","text":"Entering a new Murabaha to give a defaulting client more time for extra charge is the modern form of Bai‘ al-Kali bil Kali; all Shari’ah boards prohibit it (p. 147)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t7.16","t7.14","t9.9.6","t15.3.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What is the modern example of Al Kali bil Kali given in Section 6.10?","options":["Ijarah Sukuk","Rollover in Murabaha after default","Musharakah","Spot FX"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 147."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch6-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch6-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"6.10","sectionTitle":"Bai' al-Dayn (Sale of Debt)","title":"Bai' al-Dayn: Sale of Debt and Debt Instruments","covers":["t6.10"],"overview":"A credit document/debt cannot generally be sold at a price other than its nominal (face) value; even at face value, sale of debt is valid only with recourse to the original debtor, as under Hawalah.","simpleExplanation":"You generally can't sell a debt someone owes you -- like an invoice or a bill of exchange -- to someone else, especially not at a discount or premium, because it's too uncertain whether the original debtor will actually pay. The only way to legitimately hand a debt to someone else is through Hawalah, where the new holder can still go after the original debtor if something goes wrong.","academicExplanation":"A credit document (bill of exchange, export bill, promissory note) arising from a credit sale represents a debt, which cannot be sold under Shari'ah rules due to Gharar and/or Riba -- a trader cannot sell such a document to an Islamic bank the way he could to a conventional bank. As an alternative, the bank can act as a genuine trader, purchasing the underlying commodity from its producer and reselling it to those needing credit, keeping its own margin. The OIC Fiqh Academy and Shari'ah scholars generally regard sale/purchase of debt-representing securities/documents at any price other than face value as incompatible with Shari'ah; even at face value, sale of debt is valid only where the purchaser has recourse to the original debtor, as under Hawalah. The Fiqh maxim 'Al-Kali bil-Kali' (also termed Bai' al-Dayn bid-Dayn) forbids exchange of two delayed counter-values, prevalent among pre-Islamic Arabs -- specifically prohibiting a person who cannot pay a due credit debt from asking to 'sell it to me on credit for a further period, for something additional' (the Prophet reportedly prohibited this, and it carries near-universal Ijma'a authority). The best contemporary example of this prohibited practice is 'rollover' in Murabaha: when a client defaults, banks enter a fresh Murabaha to extend time and charge more on the receivable -- all Shari'ah boards prohibit this and any resulting return is illegitimate income. In early-1980s Pakistan, banks were briefly permitted to purchase trade bills as a form of Murabaha, but the Council of Islamic Ideology and Shari'ah Courts disapproved this practice; Islamic banks should not trade in such securities/debts since debt instruments are not saleable at a premium or discount. The key legal distinction: 'sale of debt' (prohibited) differs from 'assignment of debt' via Hawalah (permissible) in that Hawalah provides recourse to the assignor/original debtor if the assignee (new creditor) is not paid. Sukuk certificates, when genuinely backed by and evidencing ownership of a real asset (via securitization), transform into objects of value and become validly tradeable in primary and secondary markets, provided returns are tied to the cash flow of the underlying asset. Semi-debt instruments like leasing contracts (which obligate the user to specific rent commitments) resemble debt but can be traded under certain conditions since such trading represents sale of the leased asset itself at negotiated prices.","examExplanation":"Bai' al-Dayn (sale of debt) = generally prohibited (Gharar/Riba) -- can't sell a bill of exchange, export bill, or promissory note to an Islamic bank at a premium/discount. Even at face value, valid only with recourse to the original debtor (as in Hawalah). Al-Kali bil-Kali maxim: forbids exchange of two delayed counter-values (Bai' al-Dayn bid-Dayn) -- classic example being demanding a further-delayed, higher price when a credit debtor can't pay on time. Modern example of the prohibited practice: Murabaha 'rollover' on default -- prohibited by all Shari'ah boards. Sukuk are different: if genuinely asset-backed (not pure debt-representation), they ARE tradeable, since their cash flow derives from a real underlying asset.","keyPoints":["Sale of debt/debt-representing documents (bills of exchange, export bills, promissory notes) at other than face value is prohibited for Gharar/Riba.","Even at face value, sale of debt is only valid where the purchaser has recourse to the original debtor -- i.e. structured as Hawalah, not a true 'sale.'","Al-Kali bil-Kali (Bai' al-Dayn bid-Dayn): the Fiqh maxim forbidding exchange of two delayed counter-values -- specifically, extending a defaulted credit debt further in exchange for an additional amount.","Murabaha 'rollover' on client default (extending time via a fresh Murabaha and charging more) is a modern example of this prohibited practice; any resulting return is illegitimate income.","Islamic banks cannot purchase trade bills/debt instruments as a Murabaha substitute; Pakistan's Council of Islamic Ideology and Shari'ah Courts disapproved this practice when attempted in the 1980s.","'Sale of debt' (prohibited) differs from 'assignment of debt' via Hawalah (permissible) in that Hawalah preserves recourse to the original debtor if the new creditor isn't paid.","Asset-backed Sukuk, genuinely representing ownership of a real underlying asset with cash flow tied to it, are validly tradeable in primary and secondary markets -- unlike pure debt instruments.","Semi-debt instruments like leasing contracts can be traded under conditions, since such trading is really a sale of the underlying leased asset at negotiated prices."],"definitions":[{"term":"Al-Kali bil-Kali","definition":"A Fiqh maxim forbidding the sale of debt -- the exchange of two things both delayed, or one delayed counter-value for another delayed counter-value; also termed Bai' al-Dayn bid-Dayn."}],"conditions":["Sale of debt is valid at face value only where the purchaser has recourse to the original debtor (as structured via Hawalah).","Sukuk are tradeable only where genuinely backed by and evidencing ownership of a real asset, with returns tied to that asset's cash flow."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Prohibited Murabaha rollover","kind":"textbook","text":"When a Murabaha client defaults, the bank enters into a fresh Murabaha to give the client more time and charges more on the receivable -- this is a modern instance of the Al-Kali bil-Kali prohibition, condemned by all Shari'ah boards."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often think all debt-related instruments (including Sukuk) fall under the Bai' al-Dayn prohibition; the text distinguishes pure debt certificates (nontradeable) from genuinely asset-backed Sukuk (tradeable, since they represent ownership of a real, income-generating asset)."],"distinctions":["Sale of debt (prohibited except at face value with recourse) vs assignment of debt via Hawalah (permissible, preserves recourse to original debtor)","Pure debt instruments (nontradeable) vs genuinely asset-backed Sukuk (tradeable)"],"relatedConcepts":["Hawalah","Sukuk","Murabaha","Gharar"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[145,146,147]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Sale of debt","b":"Assignment of debt (Hawalah)","text":"Sale of debt is prohibited; in Hawalah there is recourse to the assignor/original debtor if the assignee does not pay."}]},{"id":"t6.11","section":"6.11","title":"Al ‘Inah Sale and the Use of Ruses (Hiyal)","pages":[147,150],"tier":"core","concepts":["inah","hiyal","tawarruq","bai-wafa"],"intuition":"If a seller sells on credit for $1000 and buys back for $950 cash, what has really happened? A loan with interest — ‘Inah.","simpleExplanation":"Hiyal are legal ruses to get round the law; Shafi‘is, Malikis and Hanbalis prohibit them, Hanafis allow only those compatible with the spirit of Shari’ah. Bai‘ al ‘Inah (sell on credit, buy back cheaper for cash) is prohibited by jurists in general. Tawarruq (selling to a third party) differs. Bai‘ bil Wafa (sale with a separate promise to resell) is allowed by Hanafis; Diminishing Musharakah uses a one-year gap to avoid ‘Inah.","academicExplanation":["Fiqh literature records ruses to circumvent Riba. Schacht studied Hiyal; acceptable Hiyal are precautionary procedural devices to avoid clashes with the law, not to avoid it. Mahmasani: texts aim at the interests served, not appearances; bypassing law is deceit; the Prophet, Companions and Followers opposed legal fictions (Ibn Masud and Ibn Abbas rejected gifts from a debtor before settlement). Shafi‘is, Malikis and Hanbalis declare Hiyal haram; Hanafis allow those compatible with Shari’ah’s spirit — e.g. a borrower selling to or buying from the lender at a different price with real purchase and sale (Tawarruq), unlike ‘Inah with buy-back.","Bai‘ al ‘Inah (Fatawa Alamgiri): selling a commodity for $1000 payable in a year and buying it back for $950 cash — a double sale netting a loan with interest; a stratagem to reach illegal ends by legal means. Ibn Qudama: buying back at less than a credit sale price, or buying on credit above a cash sale price, is impermissible. Some Shafi‘is allow ‘Inah in specific cases, but Rosly and Sanusi find no significant justification. Al Baraka rejected a company buying on credit at $20 and selling to a wholly owned sister company for $15 cash; if not fully owned, it may be permissible absent manipulation. Buy-back mark-up operations in Pakistan’s 1980s NIB system were declared non-compliant.","Another form: “Buy for me this object for 10 cash and I will buy it for 12 on credit” — not necessarily Riba if normal trade and real ownership transfer occur; Malikis prohibit it by Sadd al-Zarai‘ (blocking the means). Ibn Taymiyah: if both aim at money for more money later, it is Riba; he classifies the buyer’s intention: (1) to use goods — permitted sale; (2) to trade — permitted; (3) to get cash — Tawarruq, Makrooh per the most eminent jurists.","Bai‘ bil Wafa (Hanafi): sale of a house with the buyer’s promise to resell when the price is returned, treating the house as collateral; allowed if resale is not a condition of the first sale (a separate promise creates an enforceable right). Accordingly Islamic banks’ Diminishing Musharakah housing finance: the bank buys part of the client’s property and the client promises to repurchase after a period in which market value changes — generally one year — to avoid ‘Inah."],"examExplanation":"Hiyal: prohibited by Shafi‘i/Maliki/Hanbali; Hanafi only if consistent with spirit. ‘Inah (sell on credit, buy back cheaper for cash) prohibited — loan with interest. Tawarruq (sale to third party) differs; Ibn Taymiyah: Makrooh. Bai‘ bil Wafa allowed (Hanafi) if promise separate. DM repurchase after ~1 year to avoid ‘Inah.","keyPoints":["‘Inah = double sale with buy-back.","Sister-company buy-back is ‘Inah if wholly owned.","Sadd al-Zarai‘: blocking the means (Maliki).","Ibn Taymiyah’s three intentions."],"definitions":[{"term":"Bai‘ al ‘Inah","definition":"Double sale with buy-back: selling on credit and repurchasing for less in cash (or vice versa), netting a loan with interest."},{"term":"Hiyal (sing. Hilah)","definition":"Legal ruses/devices to make transactions technically permissible."},{"term":"Bai‘ bil Wafa","definition":"Sale with a separate promise by the buyer to resell to the seller when the price is returned (Hanafi); the asset acts as collateral."},{"term":"Sadd al-Zarai‘","definition":"Blocking the means to an illicit end."}],"examples":[{"title":"Sister company buy-back","kind":"textbook","text":"Al Baraka refused a company buying on credit for $20 and selling for $15 cash to its wholly owned holding company — it is ‘Inah. If the other company is not wholly owned and there is no manipulation, it would not be ‘Inah (p. 149)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t13.3","t12.9.2","t9.9.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A company buys a commodity on credit for $20 and sells it for $15 cash to its wholly owned sister company (the original seller). Al Baraka’s committee regarded this as:","options":["Tawarruq","Bai‘ al ‘Inah","Salam","Permissible Murabaha"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 149."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch6-t11"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch6-t11","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"6.11","sectionTitle":"Al 'Inah Sale and the Use of Ruses (Hiyal)","title":"Hiyal (Legal Ruses), Bai' al-'Inah, and Tawarruq","covers":["t6.11"],"overview":"Hiyal (legal ruses used to circumvent Riba prohibition) are generally disapproved as deceit; Bai' al-'Inah (a buy-back double sale netting to a loan with interest) is prohibited by most jurists, while Tawarruq (genuine trade with a different party) is permitted.","simpleExplanation":"Throughout history, some people tried to get around the ban on interest using clever legal tricks that technically follow the letter of the law but break its spirit. One famous example is 'Inah: selling something on credit and immediately buying it back for less in cash -- which nets out to exactly a loan with interest disguised as two sales. Most scholars say this is not allowed, because what matters is the real economic effect, not the paperwork. A similar-looking but genuinely different transaction, Tawarruq, is allowed because it involves a real third party and real ownership transfer, not a fake buy-back with the same person.","academicExplanation":"Fiqh literature (Fatawa Alamgiri, Mahmasani's Falsafa al-Tashri, Shatbi's al-Mowafaqat) documents numerous historical Hiyal (legal ruses/subterfuges) used to make Riba-avoidance transactions technically permissible. Joseph Schacht's modern scholarship links some Hiyal to casuistry that could mislead people; properly understood, legitimate Hiyal are precautionary procedural devices for framing contracts carefully to avoid clashing with the law (not to circumvent the law's substance). Mahmasani gives three grounds for prohibiting Hiyal: (1) Shari'ah texts target the underlying interest/purpose of acts, not mere appearances -- acts must be interpreted by spirit and intent; (2) bypassing the law's substance is deceit, itself prohibited; (3) the Prophet, Companions, and Followers opposed legal fictions (e.g. Ibn Masud and Ibn Abbas ruled against accepting a gift from a debtor before debt settlement, since this was really a ruse to legalize a delay-for-interest arrangement). Shafi'e, Maliki, and Hanbali schools declare Hiyal haram outright; Hanafis permit only those Hiyal compatible with the Shari'ah's spirit -- e.g. a borrower genuinely selling something to a lender below its actual price, or buying something from the lender above its actual price (this is real purchase/sale, termed Tawarruq, distinct from 'Inah's buy-back structure). Bai' al-'Inah is defined as a double sale (buy-back) whereby borrower and lender sell and resell a commodity between themselves -- once for cash, once for a higher price on credit -- netting to a loan with interest; an example cited from Fatawa Alamgiri (selling $1000 payable after a year, then buying the same item back for $950 cash) is declared unlawful for involving Riba. Ibn Qudama states: selling something on credit and then buying it back at a lower price is impermissible; selling for cash and buying back on credit at a higher price is also impermissible; but buying back at a lower cash price than the cash sale price would be permissible. Although some Shafi'es historically allowed 'Inah in specific cases, the majority (including, per a cited modern study by Rosly and Sanusi, effectively the Shafi'e position too) find no significant Shari'ah justification for it -- Al Baraka's Shari'ah committee, for instance, disapproved a company buying a commodity on credit for $20 and reselling for cash for $15 to a sister/holding company, precisely because this is Bai' al-'Inah in substance (the same seller effectively buys back). If the two companies aren't commonly owned, however, this would not constitute 'Inah, provided there's no manipulation to circumvent Riba. Ibn Taymiyah is quoted emphasizing that actions are judged by intentions: if both parties intend simply to obtain more money against a delayed larger sum, it is Riba regardless of the transaction's form. He divides 'Inah-type purchases by the buyer's intent into three categories: (1) genuine use (permitted, ordinary sale); (2) genuine trade (permitted); (3) obtaining cash via buy-and-resell because borrowing/Salam wasn't feasible -- this is Tawarruq, which Ibn Taymiyah calls Makrooh (reprehensible, though not outright haram) per most eminent jurists. A related variant, Bai' bil Wafa (Hanafi Fiqh), treats a sold asset effectively as collateral: the buyer promises to resell the house back to the seller whenever the seller returns the price -- permitted by Hanafi jurists only if the resale-back is not made a binding condition of the original sale (a mere subsequent promise is acceptable and creates an enforceable right, even if made before the first sale, provided the first sale itself carries no such condition). This underlies the one-year holding period scholars recommend for Diminishing Musharakah housing finance, so that the bank's later resale of its share doesn't fall into prohibited Bai' al-'Inah territory.","examExplanation":"Hiyal (legal ruses) generally disapproved (Shafi'e/Maliki/Hanbali: haram outright; Hanafi: only Shari'ah-spirit-compatible Hiyal permitted). Bai' al-'Inah = buy-back double sale (sell high on credit, immediately buy back low for cash, or vice versa) netting to a disguised interest loan -- prohibited by the majority (Ibn Qudama's specific rule: buying back at a LOWER cash price than what you sold for cash is fine; buying back cheaper-on-credit-after-selling-on-credit or dearer-on-credit-after-selling-cash is 'Inah, prohibited). Distinguish from Tawarruq (genuine purchase + resale to a DIFFERENT, unrelated third party -- permitted, though Ibn Taymiyah calls it Makrooh/reprehensible when its sole motive is cash-extraction). Ibn Taymiyah: intentions determine the ruling -- if both parties merely aim to get more money against a delayed larger sum, it's Riba regardless of form. Bai' bil Wafa (sell + later resale-back promise, not a binding condition) is a permitted Hanafi ruse if structured without a binding buy-back condition -- underlies Diminishing Musharakah's ~1-year holding period.","keyPoints":["Hiyal (legal ruses to circumvent Riba) are declared haram outright by Shafi'e, Maliki, and Hanbali schools; Hanafis permit only those compatible with the Shari'ah's underlying spirit.","Bai' al-'Inah: a double buy-back sale (sell high on credit, buy back low for cash, between the same two parties) that nets to a disguised interest loan -- prohibited by the majority of jurists.","Ibn Qudama's specific rule: selling on credit then buying back at a LOWER credit price is impermissible; selling cash then buying back at a HIGHER credit price is impermissible; buying back at a lower CASH price than the original cash sale is permissible.","Tawarruq (buying on credit, then reselling to a DIFFERENT, unrelated third party for cash) is distinct from 'Inah and generally permitted, though Ibn Taymiyah labels it Makrooh (reprehensible) when purely a cash-extraction device.","Ibn Taymiyah: rulings follow intentions -- if both parties merely aim to obtain more money against a larger delayed sum, it is Riba, 'no matter how it is arrived at.'","Al Baraka's Shari'ah committee disapproved a same-group-company buy-sell-back arrangement as substantively Bai' al-'Inah; a similar deal with an unrelated company would not be 'Inah, absent manipulation.","Bai' bil Wafa (a sale where the buyer later promises, without a binding condition, to resell the asset back once the seller returns the price) is a permitted Hanafi structure, provided the resale-back is not made a condition of the original sale.","The one-year holding-period convention in Diminishing Musharakah housing finance exists specifically to avoid the transaction falling into prohibited Bai' al-'Inah territory."],"definitions":[{"term":"Hiyal (Hilah, sing.)","definition":"Legal ruses or tricks used in transactions to circumvent basic Shari'ah prohibitions; some are accepted as legitimate precautionary devices, others rejected as deceit."},{"term":"Bai' al-'Inah","definition":"A double sale involving buy-back, by which a borrower and lender sell and then resell a commodity between them, once for cash and once for a higher price on credit, netting to a loan with interest."},{"term":"Bai' bil Wafa","definition":"A sale with a right of the seller to repurchase (redeem) the property by refunding the purchase price -- effectively treating the sold asset as collateral; not permissible if the resale-back is made a binding condition of the initial sale."}],"conditions":["For Bai' bil Wafa to be valid, the promise to resell back must not be a binding condition of the original sale -- a subsequent, unconditioned promise is acceptable.","For Tawarruq to avoid 'Inah-like status, the purchase and resale must involve genuinely different, unrelated parties, with real transfer of ownership."],"principles":["Ibn Taymiyah: rulings follow the parties' actual intentions, not merely the transaction's formal structure."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Fatawa Alamgiri example of prohibited 'Inah","kind":"textbook","text":"A person sells a commodity for $1000 payable after a year, then immediately buys the same commodity back for $950 cash -- declared unlawful due to the involvement of Riba (the net effect is a $950 loan repaid as $1000)."},{"title":"Disapproved sister-company buy-sell-back","kind":"textbook","text":"Al Baraka's Shari'ah committee disapproved a company buying a commodity on credit for $20 and reselling it for cash for $15 to a sister/holding company, as this is Bai' al-'Inah in substance."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often conflate Tawarruq and Bai' al-'Inah since both involve buying and reselling; the critical distinction is the counterparty -- 'Inah involves buy-back between the SAME two original parties, while Tawarruq involves resale to a genuinely different, unrelated third party.","'Inah is prohibited regardless of whether formal legal preconditions are technically met; Ibn Taymiyah's intention-based reasoning means the substance (both parties aiming to net more money against a delayed larger sum) governs the ruling, not the paperwork."],"distinctions":["Bai' al-'Inah (buy-back with the same party -- prohibited) vs Tawarruq (resale to a different party -- permitted, though Makrooh per Ibn Taymiyah when purely for cash)","A binding resale-back condition in Bai' bil Wafa (not permissible) vs a subsequent, unconditioned promise to resell back (permissible)"],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al-Dayn","Diminishing Musharakah","Wa'dah","Riba"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[147,148,149,150]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Bai‘ al ‘Inah","b":"Tawarruq","text":"In ‘Inah the commodity is bought back by the original seller; in Tawarruq the buyer sells it to a third party in a separate transaction."}],"processSteps":["Seller sells commodity to buyer on credit (e.g. $1000 in one year)","Seller immediately buys it back for cash at a lower price (e.g. $950)","Commodity returns to the seller; buyer holds $950 now and owes $1000 later","Net result: a loan with interest — therefore prohibited"]},{"id":"t6.12","section":"6.12","title":"Options in Sales (Khiyar)","pages":[150,152],"tier":"core","concepts":["khiyar"],"intuition":"What protection does a buyer have if the goods are defective or not as described?","simpleExplanation":"Khiyar is a right to cancel a sale. Main types: Khiyar al-Shart (stipulated period), al-Majlis (until parties separate), al-Ro’yat (on inspection), al-‘Aib (defect — available even without stipulation), al-Wasf (quality missing), Ghaban (price far above market) and Khiyar-e-Naqad (payment within days). These are unlike conventional ‘options’ traded as rights. In MPO the client has options of defect and specification.","academicExplanation":["The seller must disclose all defects; a buyer unaware of a defect may accept or reject. Even a valid sale may not be binding if an option is provided (Khiyar al-Shart). Khiyar al-Majlis allows cancellation until the parties leave, unless it is stipulated that the contract is final. These differ entirely from conventional options, which do not meet the conditions of a valid sale. The Prophet recommended a Companion reserve a three-day option; jurists agree on three days, differ beyond. Options for unseen goods and defects exist without stipulation; the greatest defect is lack of title. Khiyar-e-Naqad: sale annulled if payment is not made within, say, three days.","Five important options: Khiyar al-Shart (rescind within specified days; Bai‘ al Khiyar); Khiyar al Ro’yat (on inspection); Khiyar al ‘Aib (defect — available even unstipulated if not disclosed and causing a visible decrease in value; Hanafis allow a seller’s disclaimer of responsibility); Khiyar al Wasf (quality); Khiyar-e-Ghaban (price far above market presented as market price). On unseen goods: Hanafis and one Shafi‘i view — not binding even if conforming; Malikis, a Shafi‘i view and Hanbalis — binding if conforming to description (Al-Dhareer: cogent). In Salam and Istisna‘a, Khiyar al Ro’yat is not available if goods meet specifications. In MPO, the client has options of defect and specification: he may decline to buy or rescind unless the bank obtains a fitness certificate at the time of sale after giving the client opportunity to examine."],"examExplanation":"Khiyar types: Shart (stipulated, ~3 days), Majlis, Ro’yat, ‘Aib (defect, even unstipulated), Wasf, Ghaban, Naqad. Not like conventional options. Salam/Istisna‘a: no Ro’yat if specs met. MPO: options of defect and specification unless fitness certificate after examination.","keyPoints":["Three-day option recommended by the Prophet (pbuh).","Lack of title is the greatest defect.","Hanafis accept a seller’s ‘no responsibility for defects’ declaration."],"definitions":[{"term":"Khiyar","definition":"Option — the right of a party to rescind or ratify a sale in specified circumstances."}],"examples":[{"title":"Three-day option","kind":"textbook","text":"The Prophet (pbuh) advised a Companion to reserve a three-day option in all his purchases; jurists agree on three days and differ on longer periods (p. 151)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Khiyar is the same as a conventional option.","right":"Khiyar is a right to rescind a real sale for the disadvantaged party; conventional options are rights sold separately and do not meet sale conditions (p. 151)."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.4.1","t9.9.2","t8.8.8"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which option is available to a buyer even if not stipulated in the contract?","options":["Khiyar al-Shart","Khiyar al-‘Aib (defect)","Khiyar-e-Naqad","None"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 151."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch6-t12"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch6-t12","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"6.12","sectionTitle":"Options in Sales (Khiyar)","title":"Khiyar: Options to Cancel or Ratify a Sale","covers":["t6.12"],"overview":"Khiyar is the Islamic law doctrine allowing a party an option (Majlis, Shart, Ro'yat, 'Aib, Wasf, or Ghaban) to rescind or ratify a sale, protecting an informationally disadvantaged or defect-affected party.","simpleExplanation":"Islamic law gives buyers (and sometimes sellers) several kinds of built-in 'change your mind' rights: you can cancel while you're still physically together finishing the deal, you can build in a specific number of days to think it over, you can back out if the goods turn out defective or not as described, and you can back out if you were secretly overcharged. This is very different from modern financial 'options' that are bought and sold on their own as speculative bets -- Islamic Khiyar exists to protect fairness in a real sale, not to be traded as a separate product.","academicExplanation":"The Shari'ah requires sellers to disclose all defects in goods sold, or the sale is invalid; a buyer unaware of a defect (small or large) at the time of sale has the option to keep the goods at the agreed price or reject them. If a seller represents an asset as having a specific quality it lacks, the buyer may annul the contract -- though the right to exercise an option is not automatic in all forms; it must be specified where required (Khiyar al-Shart), though several other Khiyar arise automatically by law. Even a fully valid sale, free of illegality, need not be absolutely binding if an option condition (Khiyar al-Shart) exists. As long as the parties remain at the place of contract, either may cancel the deal (Khiyar al-Majlis) -- unless it's stipulated the contract is final even without separating. This concept of Khiyar is entirely distinct from conventional derivative 'options' bought/sold as standalone speculative rights, which fail the conditions for a valid sale (discussed further in Chapter 8). The Prophet recommended a Companion reserve a three-day option in all his purchases; jurists are unanimous on the validity of such an option, though they differ on periods exceeding three days. Beyond stipulated options, a purchaser automatically has an option for goods purchased unseen, and for defects in the goods -- the greatest defect being lack of title/right to sell on the seller's part. Parties may also agree the contract will be annulled if payment isn't made within a specified period (Khiyar-e-Naqad; the sale remains valid only if payment is made within that period). Five key types of Khiyar: (1) Khiyar al-Shart -- a stipulated right for either party to rescind within a specified number of days (also called Bai' al-Khiyar); (2) Khiyar al-Ro'yat -- an option exercisable upon inspecting the goods, allowing return if the goods don't match the contract, where such an option was provided; (3) Khiyar al-'Aib -- an option for defects, available automatically (even without a stipulation) if the defect wasn't disclosed and caused a visible decrease in value, though a seller's explicit at-contract disclaimer of responsibility for defects is valid per Hanafis; (4) Khiyar al-Wasf -- option of quality, where goods sold as a specified quality lack that quality, permitting return; (5) Khiyar-e-Ghaban -- option relating to price, where goods are sold far above market price while the buyer is told/led to believe he's paying the market price. On Khiyar al-Ro'yat specifically, jurists differ on whether sale of unseen items is binding: Hanafis and Shafi'is (one view) hold the sale is not binding until viewed -- the buyer may revoke or ratify upon seeing, even if the item matches the description, since not seeing it obstructs completion; Malikis, Shafi'is (another view), and Hanbalis hold the sale is binding if the item matches the earlier description, but the buyer retains the option to ratify or revoke if it differs. In Salam and Istisna'a, Khiyar al-Ro'yat is unavailable if goods conform to the pre-agreed specifications. In Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, the client retains options of defect and specification/quality -- if goods don't match specification or have a material defect, the client may decline to purchase per his promise, or (if Murabaha has already been executed) rescind the sale, unless the bank has obtained a fitness certificate at the time of sale after giving the client adequate opportunity to examine the asset.","examExplanation":"Khiyar = the doctrine of option/right of cancellation in a sale. Five key types: (1) Khiyar al-Shart -- stipulated right to rescind within specified days; (2) Khiyar al-Ro'yat -- option on inspection of goods (unseen-at-contract items); (3) Khiyar al-'Aib -- automatic option for undisclosed defects reducing value; (4) Khiyar al-Wasf -- option for missing stipulated quality; (5) Khiyar-e-Ghaban -- option where price was far above market and buyer was misled about it. Khiyar al-Majlis: either party may cancel while still at the place of contract, unless stipulated otherwise. Distinct from conventional financial 'options' (standalone tradeable rights) -- Khiyar exists within a real sale to protect fairness, not as an independent speculative instrument. In Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, the client has defect/specification options unless the bank obtains a fitness certificate after giving inspection opportunity.","keyPoints":["Khiyar = the Islamic law doctrine of option -- the right to rescind or ratify a sale, distinct from and much broader in purpose than conventional tradeable financial options.","Khiyar al-Majlis: while the parties remain at the place of contract (haven't separated), either may cancel the deal, unless stipulated as final regardless of separation.","Khiyar al-Shart: a stipulated right for either party to rescind within a specified number of days (also called Bai' al-Khiyar); the Prophet recommended reserving three days -- jurists unanimously validate this period, differing on longer periods.","Khiyar al-Ro'yat: option exercisable on inspecting previously-unseen goods; jurists differ on whether such a sale is binding before inspection.","Khiyar al-'Aib: automatic option for undisclosed defects causing a visible decrease in value -- available even without a stipulation, unless the seller explicitly disclaimed responsibility at contract time (valid per Hanafis).","Khiyar al-Wasf: option where goods sold as a specified quality turn out to lack it.","Khiyar-e-Ghaban: option where the buyer was charged far above market price while led to believe the market price was charged.","Khiyar al-Ro'yat is unavailable in Salam/Istisna'a if the delivered goods conform to the pre-agreed specifications.","In Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, the client retains defect/specification options unless the bank secures a fitness certificate after giving adequate inspection opportunity."],"definitions":[{"term":"Khiyar","definition":"Option or a power to annul or cancel a contract."},{"term":"Khiyar al-Majlis","definition":"Option for the contracting session -- the power to annul a contract possessed by both parties as long as they have not separated."},{"term":"Khiyar al-'Aib","definition":"Option of defect -- automatically available to the buyer if goods are found defective and the defect wasn't disclosed."},{"term":"Khiyar-e-Ghaban","definition":"Option relating to price -- where goods are sold at a price far higher than market price while the client is led to believe the market price was charged."}],"conditions":["Khiyar al-Shart must be specified/stipulated at the time of entering the contract to apply.","Khiyar al-'Aib requires that the defect was not disclosed to the buyer and caused a visible decrease in the commodity's value.","Khiyar al-Ro'yat is unavailable in Salam/Istisna'a where delivered goods conform to pre-agreed specifications."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Khiyar-e-Naqad (payment option)","kind":"textbook","text":"Parties may agree that if payment is not made within three days, the contract is annulled; the sale is valid only if payment is made within the specified period."}],"commonConfusions":["Khiyar (a fairness-protecting cancellation right embedded within a genuine sale) is often confused with conventional financial 'options' (standalone tradeable speculative rights); the chapter is explicit these are entirely different concepts, and conventional options fail the conditions of a valid sale.","Students sometimes think all five Khiyar types require explicit stipulation; only Khiyar al-Shart (and Khiyar-e-Naqad) require stipulation -- Khiyar al-'Aib, for instance, arises automatically from an undisclosed defect."],"distinctions":["Khiyar (Islamic law's option-to-cancel doctrine, embedded in a real sale) vs conventional derivative options (standalone tradeable rights, not valid sales per Shari'ah)","Stipulated options (Khiyar al-Shart, Khiyar-e-Naqad) vs automatically-arising options (Khiyar al-'Aib)"],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al-Khiyar","Murabaha to Purchase Orderer","Salam","Istisna'a"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150,151,152]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Khiyar in Islamic sales","b":"Conventional options","text":"Khiyar protects an informationally disadvantaged party within a genuine sale; conventional options are rights bought and sold for their own sake and do not meet valid-sale conditions."}],"table":{"caption":"Types of Khiyar (Section 6.12)","head":["Option","Right"],"rows":[["Khiyar al-Shart","Rescind within a stipulated period"],["Khiyar al-Majlis","Cancel until the parties separate"],["Khiyar al-Ro’yat","Rescind on inspection if not per contract"],["Khiyar al-‘Aib","Return defective goods (even without stipulation)"],["Khiyar al-Wasf","Return if a specified quality is absent"],["Khiyar-e-Ghaban","Rescind if charged far above market price presented as market"],["Khiyar-e-Naqad","Sale annulled if payment not made within specified days"]]}}],"questions":[{"id":"q6.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement best describes the difference between Bai‘ and a Riba-based loan?","options":["Both give a definite right of return","Bai‘ yields risk-based return; a Riba loan gives a definite right of return with excess","Loans transfer ownership permanently","Bai‘ requires equal exchange"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 131.","topicId":"t6.2","chapter":6,"section":"6.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Bai‘ from loans","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.2","pages":[130,131]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.2","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank signs a sale with a client for a car that the bank has only ordered from the manufacturer and does not yet own. The contract is:","options":["Valid Murabaha","Void — the bank can at most promise to sell","Valid if the client pays later","Salam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 136.","topicId":"t6.5.1","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply ownership rule","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","pages":[135,138]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.3","type":"tf","prompt":"According to the OIC Fiqh Council, there is a fixed maximum percentage of profit a trader may charge.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"There is no restriction, subject to Shari’ah ethics (p. 139).","topicId":"t6.5.2","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall pricing rules","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.2","pages":[138,139]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.4","type":"application","prompt":"A seller offers: ‘$10 if you pay within a month, $12 if after two months.’ The buyer accepts without choosing. What is the status?","options":["Valid","Void due to uncertainty of price, unless one alternative is agreed at conclusion","Valid at $12","Salam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 142.","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply price-certainty rule","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,142]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.5","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does the author respond to Udovitch’s view that the cash–credit price difference fulfils the same role as interest?","options":["Agrees fully","Says it overstates the case: value arises through assets, no rent on capital is created, and the fixed price cannot grow","Says credit sales are prohibited","Says interest is permitted"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 141.","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate critiques of credit pricing","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,142]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.6","type":"identify","prompt":"Selling wholesale bales unopened on the basis of an accompanying catalogue is:","options":["Mulamasah","Munabadhah","Barnamaj sale (allowed by Imam Malik)","Bai‘ al Hasat"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 144.","topicId":"t6.7","chapter":6,"section":"6.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify Gharar-related sales","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.7","pages":[143,144]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.7","type":"multi","prompt":"Which are prohibited according to Chapter 6? (Select all.)","options":["Book-out contracts settled by price difference","Bai‘ wal Salaf","Murabaha rollover","Tawarruq with a third party (per most scholars)","Bai‘ al ‘Inah"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,4],"explanation":"Most scholars declared Tawarruq permissible (p. 145).","topicId":"t6.8","chapter":6,"section":"6.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Classify prohibited sales","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.8","pages":[144,145]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.8","type":"scenario","prompt":"An exporter holds a 90-day export bill for $100,000 and asks an Islamic bank to buy it for $98,000. The bank should:","options":["Discount it","Decline — sale of debt below face value is not allowed; it could instead act as trader in the goods","Buy it at $102,000","Buy it as Salam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 146.","topicId":"t6.10","chapter":6,"section":"6.10","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Bai‘ al Dayn rules","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[146,147]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.9","type":"order","prompt":"Order the steps of a Bai‘ al ‘Inah transaction.","items":["Seller sells commodity on credit for $1000 payable in a year","Seller buys it back immediately for $950 cash","Buyer has $950 now and owes $1000 later","Net effect: an interest-bearing loan"],"explanation":"Fatawa Alamgiri example (p. 148).","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"section":"6.11","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain ‘Inah","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[147,150]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.10","type":"match","prompt":"Match each option (Khiyar) to its trigger.","pairs":[["Khiyar al-‘Aib","Undisclosed defect"],["Khiyar al-Ro’yat","Goods not as expected on inspection"],["Khiyar-e-Ghaban","Price far above market presented as market"],["Khiyar al-Shart","Stipulated period to rescind"]],"explanation":"p. 151.","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"section":"6.12","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish Khiyar types","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150,152]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.11","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why do scholars suggest roughly a one-year gap before a client repurchases the bank’s share in Diminishing Musharakah housing finance?","options":["Tax reasons","So that the market value changes and the transaction does not fall into Bai‘ al ‘Inah","To earn more rent","It is required by Salam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 150.","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"section":"6.11","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Apply ‘Inah avoidance","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[147,150]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.12","type":"short","prompt":"Explain the difference between Bai‘ al ‘Inah and Tawarruq.","keywords":["buy back","third party","loan","separate"],"correctAnswer":"In ‘Inah, the original seller buys back the commodity it sold on credit for a lower cash price, so the commodity returns and the net effect is a loan with interest — prohibited. In Tawarruq, the credit buyer sells the commodity for cash to a third party in a separate transaction; most scholars permit it (Ibn Taymiyah considered it Makrooh).","explanation":"Sections 6.8 and 6.11.","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"section":"6.11","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish ‘Inah and Tawarruq","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[147,150]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.13","type":"tf","prompt":"A credit buyer in Murabaha cannot sell the purchased asset until all instalments are paid.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"He owns it from day one and may sell it (p. 142).","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply ownership in credit sales","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,142]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.14","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to the Majallah, a sale (Bai‘) is:","options":["The exchange of money for time","The exchange of property for property with mutual consent, completed by offer and acceptance","The transfer of usufruct for rent","A gratuitous transfer"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 130.","topicId":"t6.2","chapter":6,"section":"6.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Bai‘","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.2","pages":[130,131]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.15","type":"tf","prompt":"The author accepts the argument that 2:275 covers conventional banking business because Bai‘ has a wide meaning.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"The verse permits all exchanges except those involving Riba (p. 130).","topicId":"t6.2","chapter":6,"section":"6.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate the “banking is Bai‘” argument","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.2","pages":[130,131]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.16","type":"identify","prompt":"Which Hanafi jurist said: “Trade is of two kinds: permitted, called Bai‘, and prohibited, called Riba”?","options":["Kasani","Sarakhsi","Marghinani","Ibn Abideen"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 131.","topicId":"t6.2","chapter":6,"section":"6.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Sarakhsi on trade and Riba","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.2","pages":[130,131]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.17","type":"scenario","prompt":"Wheat is exchanged for barley, with barley to be delivered next month. What is the problem?","options":["None","Riba — they are species of the same genus (edibles), so delay is not allowed","Gharar only","It is a Salam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 131.","topicId":"t6.2","chapter":6,"section":"6.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply exchange rules to commodities","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.2","pages":[130,131]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.18","type":"multi","prompt":"Why does the author justify a trader’s profit margin, even on credit? (Select all.)","options":["He provides a service in finding and buying goods","He bears risks of storage, transit, market and price","Money has an inherent right to earn","The price, once agreed, is fixed and not increased for delay"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3],"explanation":"pp. 132–133.","topicId":"t6.3.1","chapter":6,"section":"6.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Justify trade profit","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.3.1","pages":[132,133]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.19","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does earning money from money on interest affect the economy, according to Section 6.3.1?","options":["It spreads wealth evenly","It creates a rentier class receiving a growing share of output while real producers get less","It has no effect","It reduces risk for all"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 133.","topicId":"t6.3.1","chapter":6,"section":"6.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Analyse the rentier effect of interest","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.3.1","pages":[132,133]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.20","type":"match","prompt":"Match each form of sale to its description.","pairs":[["Bai‘ al Muqayaza","Barter"],["Bai‘ al Mutlaq","Goods for money"],["Bai‘ Mu’ajjal","Deferred price"],["Bai‘ Salam","Prepaid price, deferred delivery"]],"explanation":"p. 133.","topicId":"t6.4","chapter":6,"section":"6.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall forms of sale","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.4","pages":[133,134]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.21","type":"tf","prompt":"According to Hanafis and the Majallah, delivery is complete when the seller sets the goods aside for the buyer with nothing preventing him from taking them.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"Constructive possession (pp. 136–137).","topicId":"t6.5.1","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall constructive delivery","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","pages":[135,138]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.22","type":"application","prompt":"A buys a car from B but has not received actual or constructive delivery. He agrees to sell it to C. Is the sale valid?","options":["Yes","No — the sale before possession is void","Yes if C pays cash","Only with B’s signature"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 136.","topicId":"t6.5.1","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the possession rule","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","pages":[135,138]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.23","type":"comparison","prompt":"What is Ibn Taymiyah’s view on selling non-existent items?","options":["All sales of non-existent items are void","Non-existence is not itself the cause; a sale is prohibited when it involves Gharar","All are allowed","Only fruit may be sold before it exists"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 137.","topicId":"t6.5.1","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Analyse the cause of prohibition","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","pages":[135,138]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.24","type":"identify","prompt":"Which Companion bought two goats for one dinar, sold one, and returned with a goat and the dinar?","options":["Bilal","‘Urwah","Hakim ibn Hizam","Zaid ibn Aslam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 138.","topicId":"t6.5.2","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the ‘Urwah precedent","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.2","pages":[138,139]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.25","type":"scenario","prompt":"A seller tells an unaware tourist that his carpet is “at the market price” while charging three times that. When the buyer finds out, what right does he have?","options":["None","To rescind and recover his money — Ghaban-e-Fahish","Only to demand a discount","To report Riba"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 139.","topicId":"t6.5.2","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Khiyar-e-Ghaban","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.2","pages":[138,139]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.26","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to Shukani, what is the effective cause (‘Illah) of the prohibition of “two sales in one”?","options":["The credit period","The non-fixity of the price","The use of money","The number of parties"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 140.","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify the ‘Illah of two sales in one","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,142]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.27","type":"order","prompt":"Order the stages by which a credit price becomes a debt that cannot grow.","items":["Parties bargain, with cash and credit prices on offer","One price is settled before they separate","The sale is executed and ownership passes","The price becomes a debt that cannot be increased for delay"],"explanation":"pp. 139–142.","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Sequence credit pricing","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,142]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.28","type":"identify","prompt":"Which modern Grand Mufti of Saudi Arabia permitted instalment sales with a credit price above the cash price?","options":["Shaikh Abdullah ibn Baz","Shaikh Al-Dhareer","Mufti Taqi Usmani","Shaikh Qaradawi"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"p. 140.","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall contemporary rulings on credit price","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,142]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.29","type":"tf","prompt":"An unintentional delay of up to 72 hours when paying for gold by credit card is tolerated by the OIC Fiqh Academy and Al Baraka’s committee.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 143.","topicId":"t6.6","chapter":6,"section":"6.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall settlement tolerances","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.6","pages":[142,143]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.30","type":"definition","prompt":"“A person buys an asset and sells it without taking possession, only paying or receiving the difference in price” describes:","options":["Tawarruq","A book-out contract","Salam","Istijrar"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 144.","topicId":"t6.7","chapter":6,"section":"6.7","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define book-out contracts","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.7","pages":[143,144]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.31","type":"application","prompt":"A wholesaler sells sealed bales of cloth described in a detailed catalogue, without opening them. How does Imam Malik treat this?","options":["Prohibited like Mulamasah","Permitted — established wholesale practice with known description and no intended risk","Permitted only for cotton","Makruh"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 144.","topicId":"t6.7","chapter":6,"section":"6.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Gharar to catalogue sales","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.7","pages":[143,144]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.32","type":"mcq","prompt":"How did Imam Malik define Bai‘ wal Salaf?","options":["Selling on credit","“I shall buy your goods for such if you lend me such”","Selling gold for silver","Selling fruit before it ripens"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"If the loan condition is dropped, the sale is permitted (p. 145).","topicId":"t6.8","chapter":6,"section":"6.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Bai‘ wal Salaf","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.8","pages":[144,145]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.33","type":"identify","prompt":"Which scholar objects only to combining onerous and gratuitous contracts (such as sale and loan)?","options":["Ibn Taymiyah","Ibn Abideen","Imam Shafi‘i","Ibn Hazm"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"Modern scholars largely follow this view (p. 145).","topicId":"t6.8","chapter":6,"section":"6.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Ibn Taymiyah on combined contracts","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.8","pages":[144,145]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.34","type":"multi","prompt":"Which schools or scholars prohibited ‘Arbun sale, according to Al-Dhareer? (Select all.)","options":["Hanafis","Malikis","Shafi‘is","Imam Ahmad"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"Imam Ahmad approved it (p. 146).","topicId":"t6.9","chapter":6,"section":"6.9","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall views on ‘Arbun","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.9","pages":[145,146]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.35","type":"tf","prompt":"The OIC Fiqh Council and AAOIFI allow customary down-payment sales on condition that a time limit is specified.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 146.","topicId":"t6.9","chapter":6,"section":"6.9","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall modern rulings on ‘Arbun","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.9","pages":[145,146]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.36","type":"scenario","prompt":"A client defaults on a Murabaha instalment. The bank signs a new Murabaha for the same goods to give more time at a higher price. What is this?","options":["Permissible restructuring","Rollover — the modern Bai‘ al-Kali bil Kali, prohibited","Tawarruq","Ijarah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 147.","topicId":"t6.10","chapter":6,"section":"6.10","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify prohibited rollover","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[146,147]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.37","type":"comparison","prompt":"Why can Sukuk be traded while debt instruments cannot?","options":["Sukuk pay interest","Sukuk represent ownership in assets whose cash flow backs the return, so they are property of value","Sukuk are government-guaranteed","Debt is always long-term"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 147.","topicId":"t6.10","chapter":6,"section":"6.10","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Contrast Sukuk with debt instruments","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[146,147]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.38","type":"scenario","prompt":"A ruser sells a commodity for $1,000 payable in a year and immediately buys it back for $950 cash. How does Fatawa Alamgiri classify this?","options":["Lawful Tawarruq","Bai‘ al ‘Inah — unlawful due to Riba","Salam","Bai‘ bil Wafa"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 148.","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"section":"6.11","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify Bai‘ al ‘Inah","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[147,150]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.39","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which schools declared the use of Hiyal (ruses) totally prohibited?","options":["Only Hanafis","Shafi‘is, Malikis and Hanbalis; Hanafis allow only those compatible with the spirit of Shari’ah","All four allow them","None"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 148.","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"section":"6.11","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall views on Hiyal","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[147,150]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.40","type":"identify","prompt":"Which Khiyar is available even if not stipulated, when an undisclosed defect visibly reduces value?","options":["Khiyar al-Shart","Khiyar al ‘Aib","Khiyar-e-Naqad","Khiyar al-Majlis"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 151.","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"section":"6.12","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify the option for defects","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150,152]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.41","type":"tf","prompt":"In Salam and Istisna, the buyer has Khiyar al Ro’yat even when goods match the stipulated specifications.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Not available if the goods conform to specifications (p. 152).","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"section":"6.12","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply Khiyar al Ro’yat to Salam","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150,152]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q6.42","type":"short","prompt":"What options does a client have in Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, and how can the bank limit them?","keywords":["defect","specification","rescind","certificate"],"correctAnswer":"The client has the options of defect and of specification/quality: if the goods do not match the specification or have a material defect, he may refuse to buy under his promise, or rescind if the Murabaha is already executed. The bank can obtain a certificate of fitness at the time of sale after giving the client full opportunity to examine the asset.","explanation":"p. 152.","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"section":"6.12","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain client options in MPO","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150,152]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch6-1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What happens to ownership of a commodity when a sale (Bai') agreement is concluded, regardless of whether payment is cash or deferred?","explanation":"The chapter states that in trade, ownership of the subject matter transfers to the buyer as soon as the sale agreement takes place, irrespective of whether payment is cash or deferred; in the deferred case, the buyer then owes the agreed price (a debt), not the commodity.","topicId":"t6.2","chapter":6,"section":"6.2","topicLabel":"Bai' -- Exchange of Values","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Bai' and explain how it differs from Riba-based lending in terms of risk, ownership transfer, and reward.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.2","pages":[130,131]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Ownership stays with the seller until full payment is received","Ownership transfers to the buyer completely, instantly, and irreversibly","Ownership is shared between buyer and seller until the debt is settled","Ownership transfers only once the buyer resells the item"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-2","type":"scenario","prompt":"A counsel in the Pakistan Riba case argued that verse 2:275's broad use of 'Bai'' covers modern conventional banking business, making it permissible. How does the chapter respond to this argument?","explanation":"Ayub explains that while the verse does have a wider meaning for Bai' (business, earning, occupation), it also provides the general principle that all exchanges are permitted except those involving Riba -- so it doesn't validate interest-based banking merely because Bai' has broad usage.","topicId":"t6.2","chapter":6,"section":"6.2","topicLabel":"Bai' -- Exchange of Values","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Define Bai' and explain how it differs from Riba-based lending in terms of risk, ownership transfer, and reward.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.2","pages":[130]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["It agrees -- Bai' in its widest sense does legitimize conventional banking","It rejects the argument -- the verse establishes general permissibility of exchange EXCEPT where Riba is involved, not a blanket cover for interest-based banking","It says the verse is inapplicable to banking of any kind","It agrees, but only for Islamic banks, not conventional ones"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-3","type":"short","prompt":"State the Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman principle and explain how it distinguishes trade profit from Riba.","explanation":"This principle is the chapter's central justification for why trade profit is permissible while interest is not -- profit must be tied to risk-bearing.","topicId":"t6.3.1","chapter":6,"section":"6.3.1","topicLabel":"Trade versus Interest","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the Shari'ah rationale for permitting trade profit while prohibiting interest, including the Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman principle.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.3.1","pages":[132]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"definition","correctAnswer":"One can claim profit only if ready to take liability -- to bear business risk. Riba-based transactions violate this because the lender's reward is guaranteed regardless of risk, while trade profit is legitimized by the trader actually bearing business risk (storage, transit, market, price risk) and providing a genuine service."},{"id":"q-ch6-4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which TWO justifications does the chapter give for why a trader's profit margin (even a higher credit-sale margin) is legitimate?","explanation":"The chapter states the trader's margin is justified because he provides a definite service (seeking/locating/purchasing goods for the client) and because he takes business risk (damage in storage/transit, market and price risks).","topicId":"t6.3.1","chapter":6,"section":"6.3.1","topicLabel":"Trade versus Interest","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the Shari'ah rationale for permitting trade profit while prohibiting interest, including the Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman principle.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.3.1","pages":[132,133]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["He provides a definite service (seeking, locating, purchasing goods) and bears business risk (storage/transit/market/price)","He is guaranteed a fixed return by contract and takes no risk","He charges whatever the government mandates as the standard margin","He has government-granted monopoly rights over the goods"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch6-5","type":"mcq","prompt":"Match each Bai' type to its counter-value structure: (1) Bai' al-Sarf, (2) Bai' al-Muqayaza, (3) Bai' Salam -- with: (a) barter, goods for goods; (b) money/monetary units exchanged for money/monetary units; (c) immediate payment for future delivery of goods.","explanation":"Bai' al-Sarf = exchange of monetary units (b); Bai' al-Muqayaza = barter, goods for goods (a); Bai' Salam = immediate payment against future delivery (c).","topicId":"t6.4","chapter":6,"section":"6.4","topicLabel":"Types of Bai'","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"List the requirements of a valid sale contract.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.4","pages":[133]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"matching","options":["1-b, 2-a, 3-c","1-a, 2-b, 3-c","1-c, 2-a, 3-b","1-b, 2-c, 3-a"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch6-6","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Islamic bank agrees to sell client C a car currently owned by Factory F, hoping to purchase it from F and deliver it to C afterward. Is this sale valid?","explanation":"The chapter states plainly that a sale of something the seller doesn't yet own is void per Shari'ah -- the vendor must be the real owner at contract time; at most the bank can promise to sell.","topicId":"t6.5.1","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","topicLabel":"The Object of the Sale Contract","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"List the requirements of a valid sale contract, especially conditions on the object of sale (existence, ownership, possession, deliverability).","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","pages":[135,136]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, as long as the bank eventually delivers the car","No -- the seller must be the real owner of the commodity at contract time; the bank can, at most, make a promise (Wa'dah) to sell","Yes, because banks are always presumed to have implicit title","No, because such sales are always Fasid but curable retroactively"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-7","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Per Ibn Taymiyah's view discussed in the chapter, a nonexistent item cannot be sold specifically because it does not yet exist.","explanation":"False. Ibn Taymiyah's view is that a nonexistent item cannot be sold not because it is nonexistent per se, but because of the Gharar (uncertainty) this typically involves -- which is why Salam and Istisna'a (which remove Gharar via precise specification) can validly involve not-yet-existing goods.","topicId":"t6.5.1","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","topicLabel":"The Object of the Sale Contract","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"List the requirements of a valid sale contract, especially conditions on the object of sale (existence, ownership, possession, deliverability).","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","pages":[136,137]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch6-8","type":"short","prompt":"Define constructive (Hukmi) possession and give the fertilizer example from the chapter that illustrates it.","explanation":"This example demonstrates that physical transfer is not always necessary -- Hukmi (constructive) possession suffices for a valid onward resale per the Hanafi/Majallah view.","topicId":"t6.5.1","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","topicLabel":"The Object of the Sale Contract","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"List the requirements of a valid sale contract, especially conditions on the object of sale (existence, ownership, possession, deliverability).","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","pages":[137,138]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"definition","correctAnswer":"Constructive possession is when the buyer has not taken physical delivery, but the item's risk and control (and all rights/liabilities) have passed to him -- e.g. a Karachi bank buying 100,000 bags of fertilizer from a Lahore factory; once the factory sets the bags aside and gives the bank unrestricted access, the bank is the rightful, risk-bearing owner and can resell to a third party."},{"id":"q-ch6-9","type":"scenario","prompt":"A seller quotes: 'If you pay within a month, the price is $10; if you pay after two months, the price is $12,' and the buyer agrees without settling on either figure. What is the status of this sale?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly uses this exact example: as the price remains uncertain (neither alternative finally settled), the sale is void, unless one of the two alternatives is agreed upon by the parties at the time of concluding the transaction.","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","topicLabel":"Cash and Credit Prices","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain how cash and credit prices may legitimately differ, and what makes a credit-price arrangement lawful versus Riba-like.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[142]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Valid, since both prices were clearly stated","Void, because the price remains uncertain -- one of the two alternatives must be finally agreed upon at the time of concluding the transaction","Valid, but only the lower price is legally enforceable","Voidable (Fasid), curable simply by the buyer paying within one month"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-10","type":"mcq","prompt":"Per the majority view (mainly Shafi'e and Hanafi schools) discussed in the chapter, why is a single, higher credit price (agreed once at contract time) considered lawful rather than Riba?","explanation":"The chapter explains that this form is deemed lawful because the seller stipulates the whole price at the time of the bargain in return for the sale item; the difference in price is in lieu of the item of sale, not as a reward for time. Once stipulated, the price must not be changed due to the payment period.","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","topicLabel":"Cash and Credit Prices","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how cash and credit prices may legitimately differ, and what makes a credit-price arrangement lawful versus Riba-like.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,140]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because credit sales are always tax-exempt","Because the seller stipulates the whole price at the time of the bargain in return for the sale item -- the difference is in lieu of the item, not a reward for time per se","Because Islamic law has no rule against charging for time value of money in any context","Because the buyer always has the right to renegotiate the price later"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-11","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Ayub agrees with Abraham Udovitch's claim that the difference between cash and credit prices fulfils the same economic role as interest.","explanation":"False. Ayub explicitly states Udovitch 'overstates the case' -- Islamic economics ties value to the conversion of money into an asset (whose worth may rise or fall over time), not to a time-based rent on money itself, as interest does.","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","topicLabel":"Cash and Credit Prices","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how cash and credit prices may legitimately differ, and what makes a credit-price arrangement lawful versus Riba-like.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[141]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch6-12","type":"short","prompt":"A trader wants to exchange 10kg of wheat for 12kg of wheat of a different quality grade, with delivery of the 12kg delayed by one week. Is this permissible?","explanation":"The governing Hadith is explicit: wheat for wheat must be equal and hand-to-hand; if commodities differ (different genus), any quantity and delayed delivery is fine, but same-genus exchanges (like wheat for wheat) require strict equality and immediacy.","topicId":"t6.6","chapter":6,"section":"6.6","topicLabel":"Riba Involvement in Sales","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the Shari'ah rationale for permitting trade profit while prohibiting interest, including the Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman principle.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.6","pages":[142,143]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"No. Since both sides are the same genus (wheat), the Hadith rule requires like-for-like, equal-for-equal, hand-to-hand exchange -- any inequality in weight or any delay creates Riba Al-Fadl, regardless of quality grade differences."},{"id":"q-ch6-13","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the chapter's distinction between Gharar and ordinary business risk?","explanation":"The chapter is explicit: 'all risks are not Gharar, because business risk is not only a part of life but also a valid requirement for taking a return in exchanges.' Gharar concerns uncertainty regarding the subject matter's existence, definition, or deliverability.","topicId":"t6.7","chapter":6,"section":"6.7","topicLabel":"Gharar -- A Cause of Prohibition of Sales","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify Gharar-based prohibited sales and the classical examples used to illustrate them.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.7","pages":[143]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["All risk in a transaction is Gharar and therefore prohibited","Ordinary business risk (e.g. price, storage, transit risk) is not Gharar and is actually necessary for legitimately earning a return; Gharar specifically concerns excessive uncertainty about the existence/definition/deliverability of subject matter or price","Gharar only applies to currency exchange transactions","Gharar and business risk are identical terms used interchangeably in the text"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-14","type":"short","prompt":"Two people exchange garments by each throwing one to the other, without either examining what they're getting. Name this specific prohibited Gharar-based sale form.","explanation":"Munabadhah is defined in the chapter (via Imam Malik) as when a man throws to another a garment in exchange for a garment the other throws to him, without both of them examining them -- prohibited for Gharar.","topicId":"t6.7","chapter":6,"section":"6.7","topicLabel":"Gharar -- A Cause of Prohibition of Sales","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify Gharar-based prohibited sales and the classical examples used to illustrate them.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.7","pages":[143,144]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"identify","correctAnswer":"Bai' al-Munabadhah"},{"id":"q-ch6-15","type":"scenario","prompt":"A trader buys 1,000 shares and, minutes later, sells them without ever taking possession, netting only the difference between purchase and sale prices. According to the chapter, what is this transaction called and what is its status?","explanation":"The chapter names this a 'book-out contract' -- buying and selling without taking possession, settling only price differences -- common in stock/commodity/forex markets and declared prohibited by Shari'ah committees.","topicId":"t6.7","chapter":6,"section":"6.7","topicLabel":"Gharar -- A Cause of Prohibition of Sales","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify Gharar-based prohibited sales and the classical examples used to illustrate them.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.7","pages":[144]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["A valid Musawamah sale, permitted","A book-out contract -- prohibited, as it involves speculation and no real exchange of ownership","A valid Bai' al-Sarf transaction","A permissible Tawarruq arrangement"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-16","type":"mcq","prompt":"What condition does Ibn Taymiyah use to decide whether a stipulation/condition in a sale should be rejected?","explanation":"Ibn Taymiyah's relatively liberal approach: reject only stipulations which contradict a clear provision of the Qur'an, the Sunnah, or the Ijma'a, or which contradict the very object of the contract, nullifying it.","topicId":"t6.8","chapter":6,"section":"6.8","topicLabel":"Conditional Sales and Two Bargains in One Sale","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the Shari'ah rationale for permitting trade profit while prohibiting interest, including the Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman principle.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.8","pages":[145]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Any condition not explicitly named in classical Fiqh books is automatically void","Only conditions that contradict a clear provision of the Qur'an, Sunnah, or scholarly consensus (Ijma'a), or that contradict the very object of the contract","Only conditions that a majority vote of local traders disapproves of","All conditions are void unless a judge specifically approves them"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-17","type":"scenario","prompt":"A says to B: 'I shall purchase your goods for $500 if you lend me $1000.' If both parties agree to this as stated, what is the ruling?","explanation":"This matches Imam Malik's definition of Bai' wal Salaf exactly as given in the chapter: such a bundled transaction is not permitted; however, if the party who stipulated the loan abandons that stipulation, the sale is permitted.","topicId":"t6.8","chapter":6,"section":"6.8","topicLabel":"Conditional Sales and Two Bargains in One Sale","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the Shari'ah rationale for permitting trade profit while prohibiting interest, including the Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman principle.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.8","pages":[145]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Permitted, since both parties freely consented","Not permitted -- this is Bai' wal Salaf, an invalid combination of sale and loan; but if the loan-stipulating party abandons that condition, the sale itself is permitted","Permitted, as long as the loan amount exceeds the sale price","Not permitted under any circumstances, even if the loan condition is dropped"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-18","type":"comparison","prompt":"Which school of Fiqh is described in the chapter as accepting Bai' al-'Arbun as valid, against the majority who prohibit it?","explanation":"The majority (Hanafis, Malikis, Shafi'es, Zaidi Shiites, and even some Hanbalis like Abul Khattab) prohibit 'Arbun for Gharar; it was approved by Imam Ahmad (mainstream Hanbali position), citing precedent from 'Umar and several Tabi'een.","topicId":"t6.9","chapter":6,"section":"6.9","topicLabel":"Bai' al-'Arbun","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain Bai' al-'Arbun, Bai' al-Dayn, and Bai' al-'Inah, and why each is contested or restricted.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.9","pages":[145,146]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Hanafi","Maliki","Shafi'e","Hanbali"],"correctAnswer":3},{"id":"q-ch6-19","type":"short","prompt":"What condition do the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council and AAOIFI attach to permitting customary Bai' al-'Arbun sale?","explanation":"Both bodies allow customary downpayment ('Arbun) sale on the condition that a time limit is specified, addressing part of the classical Gharar concern.","topicId":"t6.9","chapter":6,"section":"6.9","topicLabel":"Bai' al-'Arbun","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain Bai' al-'Arbun, Bai' al-Dayn, and Bai' al-'Inah, and why each is contested or restricted.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.9","pages":[146]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"A time limit must be specified for the buyer's decision on whether to complete the purchase."},{"id":"q-ch6-20","type":"scenario","prompt":"A trader holds a bill of exchange representing a debt owed to him by a customer. He wants to sell this bill to an Islamic bank at a discount to get immediate cash. What does the chapter say about this?","explanation":"The chapter is explicit that a trader cannot sell such a debt document to an Islamic bank as he could to a conventional bank; instead, the bank could act as a trader, purchasing the underlying commodity from its producer and reselling on credit itself.","topicId":"t6.10","chapter":6,"section":"6.10","topicLabel":"Bai' al-Dayn (Sale of Debt)","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain Bai' al-'Arbun, Bai' al-Dayn, and Bai' al-'Inah, and why each is contested or restricted.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[146,147]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["This is permissible, just like it would be with a conventional bank","This is not permissible -- sale of debt/debt documents at other than face value is prohibited for Gharar/Riba; the bank could instead act as a genuine trader in the underlying commodity","This is permissible only if the discount rate is below 5%","This is permissible as long as the bank is the sole buyer in the market"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-21","type":"comparison","prompt":"What is the key legal difference between the prohibited 'sale of debt' and the permissible 'assignment of debt' (Hawalah)?","explanation":"The chapter states the key difference: in Hawalah (assignment), there is recourse to the assignor/original debtor if the assignee is not paid, whereas a pure 'sale of debt' lacks this and is therefore Gharar-laden and prohibited.","topicId":"t6.10","chapter":6,"section":"6.10","topicLabel":"Bai' al-Dayn (Sale of Debt)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain Bai' al-'Arbun, Bai' al-Dayn, and Bai' al-'Inah, and why each is contested or restricted.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[147]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["There is no real difference; both are prohibited","In Hawalah, the assignee has recourse to the original debtor if the new debtor fails to pay; a pure sale of debt provides no such recourse","Hawalah always occurs at a discount, while sale of debt occurs at face value","Sale of debt requires a written contract; Hawalah does not"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-22","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why are genuinely asset-backed Sukuk tradeable in secondary markets, unlike pure debt instruments?","explanation":"The chapter explains that when a certificate is supported by an asset (via securitization), it becomes an object of value and therefore qualifies for trade in primary and secondary markets, provided the return is based on cash flow from the underlying asset.","topicId":"t6.10","chapter":6,"section":"6.10","topicLabel":"Bai' al-Dayn (Sale of Debt)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain Bai' al-'Arbun, Bai' al-Dayn, and Bai' al-'Inah, and why each is contested or restricted.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[147]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because Sukuk are government-guaranteed","Because they represent ownership of a real underlying asset, transforming them into an object of value, with returns tied to the asset's cash flow","Because Sukuk holders have no rights to the underlying asset","Because Sukuk are always issued at face value with no premium or discount"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-23","type":"scenario","prompt":"A person sells a commodity for $1000 payable after a year, and then immediately buys the SAME commodity back from the same buyer for $950 cash. What is this transaction and its ruling?","explanation":"This is the exact example given from Fatawa Alamgiri in the chapter, declared unlawful as Bai' al-'Inah -- a buy-back netting to a disguised interest loan.","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"section":"6.11","topicLabel":"Al-'Inah Sale and the Use of Ruses","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain Bai' al-'Arbun, Bai' al-Dayn, and Bai' al-'Inah, and why each is contested or restricted.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[148]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["This is Tawarruq, and it is generally permitted","This is Bai' al-'Inah, declared unlawful due to the involvement of Riba (it nets to a $950 loan repaid as $1000)","This is a valid Musawamah transaction","This is Bai' al-Sarf, requiring hand-to-hand exchange only"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-24","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does Tawarruq differ from Bai' al-'Inah in structure, and what did Ibn Taymiyah call Tawarruq when its sole purpose is obtaining cash?","explanation":"The key structural difference is the counterparty: 'Inah is a buy-back between the same two original parties (prohibited); Tawarruq involves a genuinely different third-party buyer. Ibn Taymiyah's third category of 'Inah-type buyer intent (cash-extraction via buy-and-resell) is Tawarruq, which he calls Makrooh (reprehensible) per most eminent jurists.","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"section":"6.11","topicLabel":"Al-'Inah Sale and the Use of Ruses","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain Bai' al-'Arbun, Bai' al-Dayn, and Bai' al-'Inah, and why each is contested or restricted.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[149,150]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Tawarruq involves the same two original parties buying back the item; Ibn Taymiyah called it Haram","Tawarruq involves reselling to a genuinely different, unrelated third party (not a buy-back with the same party); Ibn Taymiyah called it Makrooh (reprehensible)","There is no structural difference between them","Tawarruq is only used for real estate transactions; 'Inah is only for commodities"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-25","type":"short","prompt":"Define Bai' bil Wafa and state its key condition for validity.","explanation":"Bai' bil Wafa is a permitted Hanafi ruse structure, distinct from the prohibited Bai' al-'Inah, as long as the buy-back is a separate promise rather than a binding contractual condition of the first sale.","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"section":"6.11","topicLabel":"Al-'Inah Sale and the Use of Ruses","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain Bai' al-'Arbun, Bai' al-Dayn, and Bai' al-'Inah, and why each is contested or restricted.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[149,150]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"definition","correctAnswer":"A sale with a right of the seller to repurchase (redeem) the property by refunding the purchase price -- effectively treating the sold asset as collateral until repayment. It is valid (per Hanafi jurists) only if the resale-back is not made a binding condition of the original sale; a subsequent, unconditioned promise to resell back is acceptable."},{"id":"q-ch6-26","type":"short","prompt":"Why do scholars recommend roughly a one-year holding period before an Islamic bank resells its ownership share back in Diminishing Musharakah housing finance?","explanation":"This directly connects the chapter's Bai' al-'Inah discussion to real-world Islamic banking product design.","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"section":"6.11","topicLabel":"Al-'Inah Sale and the Use of Ruses","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain Bai' al-'Arbun, Bai' al-Dayn, and Bai' al-'Inah, and why each is contested or restricted.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[150]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"To avoid the transaction falling into prohibited Bai' al-'Inah (sale-and-buyback) territory -- the passage of time and change in market value during the holding period helps distinguish it from an immediate, disguised buy-back arrangement."},{"id":"q-ch6-27","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are among the five Khiyar types named in the chapter?","explanation":"The five named types are Khiyar al-Shart, Khiyar al-Ro'yat, Khiyar al-'Aib, Khiyar al-Wasf, and Khiyar-e-Ghaban. 'Khiyar al-Ijarah' is not one of the named types.","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"section":"6.12","topicLabel":"Options in Sales (Khiyar)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe the main types of Khiyar (option) available to parties in a sale contract.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[151]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Khiyar al-Shart","Khiyar al-'Aib","Khiyar al-Ijarah","Khiyar-e-Ghaban","Khiyar al-Wasf"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3,4]},{"id":"q-ch6-28","type":"scenario","prompt":"A buyer purchases fabric that turns out to have a hidden defect, not disclosed at the time of sale, which noticeably reduces its value. The contract contained no explicit option clause. Can the buyer still return the goods?","explanation":"Khiyar al-'Aib is available automatically (without needing to be stipulated) if the defect was not disclosed and caused a visible decrease in the value of the commodity -- unlike Khiyar al-Shart, which requires explicit stipulation.","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"section":"6.12","topicLabel":"Options in Sales (Khiyar)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Describe the main types of Khiyar (option) available to parties in a sale contract.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[151]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No, since no Khiyar was explicitly stipulated in the contract","Yes -- Khiyar al-'Aib (option of defect) is automatically available to the buyer even without a stipulation, since the defect wasn't disclosed and caused a visible decrease in value","No, defects can only be claimed within the Majlis (session)","Yes, but only if the seller explicitly guaranteed no defects"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch6-29","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Khiyar (the Islamic law option to cancel a sale) is essentially the same concept as a conventional financial 'option' bought and sold in derivatives markets.","explanation":"False. The chapter explicitly distinguishes these: Khiyar is a fairness-protecting right embedded within a genuine sale of real goods, while conventional derivative options are standalone tradeable speculative rights that fail the conditions required for a valid Islamic sale.","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"section":"6.12","topicLabel":"Options in Sales (Khiyar)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the main types of Khiyar (option) available to parties in a sale contract.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150,151]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch6-30","type":"scenario","prompt":"In a Murabaha to Purchase Orderer transaction, the goods delivered to the client do not match the specification the client originally requested. What recourse does the client have, per the chapter?","explanation":"The chapter states the client retains options of defect and specification/quality in Murabaha to Purchase Orderer; he may decline per his promise, or rescind an executed sale, unless the bank secured a fitness certificate after giving adequate inspection opportunity.","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"section":"6.12","topicLabel":"Options in Sales (Khiyar)","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Describe the main types of Khiyar (option) available to parties in a sale contract.","source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[152]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["None, since the promise to purchase was binding","The client may decline to purchase per his promise (if not yet executed), or rescind the sale if already executed -- unless the bank obtained a fitness certificate after giving the client adequate opportunity to examine the asset","The client must accept the goods and can only complain informally","The client can only get a partial refund, not rescission"],"correctAnswer":1}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f6.1","front":"Bai‘","back":"Exchange of property for property with mutual consent via offer and acceptance, giving the buyer permanent ownership against the price.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t6.2","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.2","pages":[130,131]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.2","front":"Two justifications for a trader’s profit margin","back":"(1) Service of seeking, locating and purchasing goods; (2) bearing business risk — storage, transit, market and price risk.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t6.3.1","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.3.1","pages":[132,133]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.3","front":"Can a bank sell a car it has not yet bought from the factory?","back":"No — the sale is void; it can only promise to sell. Sale must follow ownership and (actual or constructive) possession.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t6.5.1","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","pages":[135,138]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.4","front":"Constructive possession example (Ch. 6)","back":"A Karachi bank buying fertiliser set aside for it by a Lahore factory owns and can sell it, bearing asset, market and price risk until sold.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t6.5.1","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","pages":[135,138]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.5","front":"Inferred maximum profit rates in Fiqh (Majallah)","back":"5% for wares, 10% for animals, 20% for real estate — though jurists generally do not recommend specific rates.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t6.5.2","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.2","pages":[138,139]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.6","front":"Credit price vs cash price","back":"Credit price may exceed cash price if one price is settled before separation; what is prohibited is increasing it after it is fixed for delay.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,142]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.7","front":"Shukani on ‘two sales in one’","back":"The ‘Illah of the prohibition is non-fixity of the price; accepting one definite price is fine.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,142]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.8","front":"Book-out contract","back":"Buying and selling without taking possession, settling only price differences — prohibited (commodities, stocks, FX).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t6.7","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.7","pages":[143,144]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.9","front":"Bai‘ wal Salaf","back":"Combining sale and loan (e.g. “I’ll buy your goods if you lend me money”) — unlawful; can hide compensation for a loan.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t6.8","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.8","pages":[144,145]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.10","front":"Al Kali bil Kali","back":"Exchange of debt for debt (both counter values delayed) — prohibited by consensus. Modern form: Murabaha rollover.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t6.10","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[146,147]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.11","front":"Sale of debt vs Hawalah","back":"Sale of debt prohibited (Gharar/Riba); Hawalah permissible because there is recourse to the assignor/original debtor.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t6.10","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[146,147]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.12","front":"Bai‘ al ‘Inah","back":"Sell on credit (e.g. $1000 in a year) and buy back for less cash ($950) — a loan with interest; prohibited by jurists in general.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[147,150]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.13","front":"Bai‘ bil Wafa","back":"Sale with a separate promise to resell when the price is returned (Hanafi); asset serves as collateral. Resale must not be a condition of the sale.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[147,150]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.14","front":"Ibn Taymiyah’s classification of buyers in ‘Inah-type sales","back":"Buy to use — permitted; buy to trade — permitted; buy to get cash (Tawarruq) — Makrooh per eminent jurists.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[147,150]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.15","front":"Khiyar al-‘Aib","back":"Option to return defective goods — available even if not stipulated, where the defect was not disclosed and visibly reduces value.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150,152]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.16","front":"Client’s options in MPO","back":"Options of defect and specification — he may refuse to buy or rescind unless the bank gets a fitness certificate at sale after allowing examination.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150,152]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.17","front":"Order of 2:275 — trade before Riba","back":"Permission of trade is mentioned before the prohibition of Riba, signalling that trade is the alternative to Riba.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t6.3.1","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.3.1","pages":[132,133]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.18","front":"When may one article be sold at two prices?","back":"Cash and credit prices may be quoted, but one must be settled before the parties separate; otherwise the sale is void.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,142]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.19","front":"Udovitch’s claim and the author’s reply","back":"Udovitch: credit mark-up performs the role of interest. Author: it prices an asset, not money; no rent on capital and no increase after the sale.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,142]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.20","front":"Can a Murabaha buyer resell before paying all instalments?","back":"Yes — he owns the asset from day one and remains liable for the debt.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,142]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.21","front":"Mulamasah and Munabadhah (Imam Malik)","back":"Touching a garment without unfolding it; throwing garments to each other without examining them — sales of risk.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t6.7","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.7","pages":[143,144]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.22","front":"Barnamaj","back":"A catalogue/list of contents; selling bales by Barnamaj without unfolding was allowed by Imam Malik.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t6.7","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.7","pages":[143,144]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.23","front":"Tawarruq (Ch. 6)","back":"Buying on credit and separately selling to another party for cash; permissible per most scholars as the contracts are not conditioned on each other; Ibn Taymiyah called it Makruh.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t6.8","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.8","pages":[144,145]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.24","front":"Sadd al-Zarai‘","back":"Blocking the means to an illicit end — the Maliki basis for prohibiting some ‘Inah-type sales.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[147,150]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.25","front":"Khiyar-e-Naqad","back":"Option of payment: the contract is annulled if payment is not made within a specified number of days.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150,152]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f6.26","front":"Five important options (Khiyar)","back":"Al-Shart (stipulated), al-Ro’yat (inspection), al-‘Aib (defect), al-Wasf (quality), Ghaban (price).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150,152]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-1","front":"Define Bai' as used in Islamic commercial law.","back":"Giving ownership of a commodity (Mabi') to another person in compensation for a price (Thaman); the seller transfers ownership on a permanent basis in exchange for the price.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t6.2","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.2","pages":[130]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-2","front":"What is the key structural difference between Bai' and a loan, in terms of ownership transfer?","back":"In Bai', ownership transfers completely, instantly and irreversibly (permanently). In a loan, ownership transfers temporarily -- the borrower must repay a similar item or the same amount of money.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t6.2","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.2","pages":[130,131]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-3","front":"State the Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman principle and its role in justifying trade profit.","back":"One can claim profit only if ready to take liability -- to bear business risk. Trade profit is justified because the trader bears storage/transit/market/price risk, unlike Riba where reward is guaranteed with no risk to the lender.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t6.3.1","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.3.1","pages":[132]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-4","front":"What two objections against Islamic banks' trading activities does the chapter address and reject?","back":"1) Banks, as intermediaries, shouldn't engage in trading/real-sector activity. 2) Charging more than the cash price is equivalent to interest. Both are rejected -- banking structure isn't fixed to one form, and credit-price premiums are justified by risk-bearing and service, not interest.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t6.3.1","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.3.1","pages":[132]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-5","front":"Distinguish Bai' al-Mutlaq, Bai' al-Sarf, Bai' al-Muqayaza, Bai' Mu'ajjal, and Bai' Salam.","back":"Bai' al-Mutlaq: goods for money (spot or credit, most common). Bai' al-Sarf: money/monetary units for money/monetary units. Bai' al-Muqayaza: barter (goods for goods). Bai' Mu'ajjal: deferred price payment (credit sale). Bai' Salam: immediate payment, deferred delivery (forward sale).","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t6.4","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.4","pages":[133]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-6","front":"Why is selling a car the seller doesn't yet own (but hopes to acquire) void?","back":"The object must exist and be owned by the seller at contract time; a sale of something not yet owned is void. At most, the seller can make a promise (Wa'dah) to sell it later once he owns it.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t6.5.1","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","pages":[135,136]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-7","front":"Define constructive (Hukmi) possession in the context of onward resale.","back":"A situation where the seller has set the item aside for the buyer, giving him unrestricted access to take physical delivery whenever he wishes -- even without actual physical transfer, this suffices as possession for a valid onward resale per the Hanafi/Majallah view.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t6.5.1","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","pages":[137,138]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-8","front":"Per Ibn Taymiyah, why exactly is selling a nonexistent item (e.g. an unborn calf) prohibited?","back":"Not because the item is nonexistent per se, but because of the Gharar (uncertainty) this typically involves. This is why Salam and Istisna'a -- which remove Gharar via precise specification -- can validly involve not-yet-existing goods.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t6.5.1","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","pages":[136,137]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-9","front":"Can a client who bought a car via Murabaha (5-year instalments) resell it after one year, before finishing payment?","back":"Yes. He owns the car from day one and is merely liable to the bank for the agreed payment schedule -- this is not 'selling what he doesn't own.'","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t6.5.1","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.1","pages":[142]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-10","front":"Define Ghaban-e-Fahish.","back":"Excessive profiteering with deception -- a seller states or implies he's charging the market price while actually charging an exorbitant price, exploiting the buyer's ignorance. The defrauded buyer may rescind the contract.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t6.5.2","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.2","pages":[139]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-11","front":"Under what two conditions is a higher credit price (than cash price) legitimate, per the chapter?","back":"1) One definite, final price must be fixed at the time of the bargain (before the parties separate). 2) That price must never later be increased due to delay in payment -- doing so would recreate Riba.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,140,141]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-12","front":"What does Sarakhsi say about selling on credit?","back":"'Selling on credit is an absolute feature of trade' -- and the most conducive means for achieving an investor's profit goal, since profit is most often achieved by selling on credit rather than for cash.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[140,141]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-13","front":"Does Ayub agree with Udovitch's claim that the cash/credit price gap functions like interest?","back":"No -- Ayub explicitly rejects this. He argues Islamic economics ties value to converting money into an asset (whose worth can rise or fall over time), not to a time-based rent charged on money itself.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[141]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-14","front":"What Hadith governs Riba Al-Fadl in the exchange of same-genus goods, and what does it require?","back":"'Gold for gold, silver for silver, wheat for wheat, barley for barley, dates for dates, salt for salt -- like for like, equal for equal, and hand to hand; if the commodities differ, sell as you wish, provided the exchange is hand to hand.' Same-genus items require exact equality and spot delivery.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t6.6","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.6","pages":[142,143]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-15","front":"Define Gharar and clarify how it differs from ordinary business risk.","back":"Gharar = hazard/uncertainty about the existence, definition, deliverability or outcome of the subject matter or price. Ordinary business risk (e.g. price/storage/transit risk) is NOT Gharar -- it's actually necessary for legitimately earning profit.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t6.7","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.7","pages":[143]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-16","front":"Distinguish Mulamasah from Munabadhah.","back":"Mulamasah: touching/feeling a wrapped garment without unfolding or examining it, as the basis for sale. Munabadhah: two parties throwing garments to each other in exchange, without either examining them. Both prohibited for Gharar.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t6.7","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.7","pages":[143,144]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-17","front":"What is a 'book-out' contract, and what is its Shari'ah status?","back":"Buying an asset and selling it again without taking possession, only netting the price difference -- common in commodities, stocks and forex. Prohibited by Shari'ah committees as it involves speculation and no real exchange of ownership.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t6.7","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.7","pages":[144]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-18","front":"What is Bai' wal Salaf, and why is it prohibited?","back":"A conditional contract combining selling and lending, e.g. 'I purchase your goods for X if you lend me Y.' Prohibited because bundling a sale with a loan condition can hide illegal compensation for the loan (Riba).","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t6.8","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.8","pages":[145]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-19","front":"What was Ibn Taymiyah's approach to rejecting stipulations/conditions in sales?","back":"He rejected only stipulations that contradict a clear provision of the Qur'an, Sunnah, or scholarly consensus (Ijma'a), or that contradict the very object of the contract -- a relatively liberal, purpose-focused approach.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t6.8","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.8","pages":[145]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-20","front":"Define Bai' al-'Arbun.","back":"A sale of downpayment where, if the buyer takes the commodity, the downpayment becomes part of the price; if he doesn't purchase, the advance money is forfeited to the seller.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t6.9","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.9","pages":[145]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-21","front":"What is the contemporary (OIC Fiqh Council / AAOIFI) position on Bai' al-'Arbun?","back":"Customary 'Arbun sale is permitted, on condition that a time limit is specified for the buyer's decision -- despite the majority classical view (Hanafi, Maliki, Shafi'e) prohibiting it for Gharar.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t6.9","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.9","pages":[146]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-22","front":"State the Al-Kali bil-Kali maxim.","back":"A Fiqh maxim forbidding the sale of debt -- the exchange of two things both delayed, or one delayed counter-value for another delayed counter-value (also termed Bai' al-Dayn bid-Dayn).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t6.10","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[146,147]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-23","front":"Under what condition is sale of a debt permissible even at face value?","back":"Only where the purchaser has recourse to the original debtor if the debtor defaults -- i.e. structured as Hawalah (assignment of debt), not a true 'sale of debt.'","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t6.10","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[146,147]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-24","front":"What is 'rollover' in Murabaha, and why is it prohibited?","back":"When a client defaults, the bank enters into a fresh Murabaha to give the client more time and charges more on the receivable. This is a modern instance of the Al-Kali bil-Kali (sale of debt for a further-delayed larger amount) prohibition -- condemned by all Shari'ah boards.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t6.10","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[147]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-25","front":"Why are genuinely asset-backed Sukuk tradeable while pure debt instruments are not?","back":"Asset-backed Sukuk represent ownership of a real underlying asset, with returns tied to its cash flow -- making them objects of value. Pure debt instruments merely represent an obligation to pay, which is nontradeable Bai' al-Dayn.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t6.10","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[147]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-26","front":"Define Bai' al-'Inah.","back":"A double sale involving buy-back, by which a borrower and lender sell and then resell a commodity between them -- once for cash, once for a higher price on credit -- netting to a loan with interest.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[148]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-27","front":"Distinguish Bai' al-'Inah from Tawarruq.","back":"'Inah involves a buy-back between the SAME two original parties (prohibited by the majority). Tawarruq involves buying on credit and reselling to a genuinely DIFFERENT, unrelated third party for cash (generally permitted, though Ibn Taymiyah calls it Makrooh/reprehensible).","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[149,150]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-28","front":"What is Ibn Qudama's specific rule for when a buy-back after a sale is or isn't 'Inah?","back":"Selling on credit then buying back at a LOWER credit price = impermissible. Selling for cash then buying back at a HIGHER credit price = impermissible. Buying back at a LOWER cash price than the original cash sale = permissible.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[148,149]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-29","front":"Define Bai' bil Wafa, and state its key validity condition.","back":"A sale where the seller later (via a separate, non-binding promise) can repurchase the property by refunding the price -- treating the asset as collateral. Valid only if the resale-back is NOT made a binding condition of the original sale.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[149,150]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-30","front":"Why do scholars recommend a roughly one-year holding period in Diminishing Musharakah housing finance?","back":"So that the bank's later repurchase/sale of its ownership share doesn't fall into prohibited Bai' al-'Inah (sale-and-buyback) territory.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[150]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-31","front":"List the five key types of Khiyar (options) discussed in the chapter.","back":"Khiyar al-Shart (stipulated right to rescind within days), Khiyar al-Ro'yat (option on inspection of unseen goods), Khiyar al-'Aib (option for undisclosed defects), Khiyar al-Wasf (option for missing stipulated quality), Khiyar-e-Ghaban (option for price far above market, buyer misled).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[151]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-32","front":"How does Khiyar (Islamic option) differ from a conventional financial 'option'?","back":"Khiyar is a fairness-protecting cancellation/ratification right embedded within a genuine sale of real goods. Conventional financial options are standalone tradeable speculative rights, which fail the conditions of a valid Islamic sale.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150,151]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch6-33","front":"Define Khiyar al-Majlis.","back":"The option for the contracting session -- as long as the parties to a contract remain at the place of contract (have not separated), either may cancel the deal, unless it is stipulated that the contract is final regardless of separation.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e6.1","kind":"long","prompt":"Explain the requirements of a valid sale in Islamic commercial law and their relevance to Islamic banks’ trade-based financing.","expectedStructure":["Define Bai‘","Parties and consent","Object: lawful, existent, owned, possessed, deliverable","Constructive possession and examples","Price certainty; cash vs credit price","Implications: promise before ownership; no rollover; no sale of debt"],"keyConcepts":["Mabi‘","Thaman","Qabza","Hukmi possession"],"essentialPoints":["Figure 6.2 elements","Fertiliser example","One price fixed"],"commonMistakes":["Requiring physical delivery in all cases","Allowing price increase on default"],"topicId":"t6.5","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5","pages":[133,135]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e6.2","kind":"difference","prompt":"Differentiate between Bai‘ al ‘Inah, Tawarruq and Bai‘ bil Wafa.","expectedStructure":["Define each","Parties and flow","Juristic position","Bank relevance (DM, liquidity)"],"keyConcepts":["buy-back","Hiyal","Sadd al-Zarai‘"],"essentialPoints":["‘Inah prohibited","Tawarruq permitted by most / Makrooh per Ibn Taymiyah","Wafa: separate promise"],"commonMistakes":["Treating Tawarruq and ‘Inah as identical"],"topicId":"t6.11","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.11","pages":[147,150]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e6.3","kind":"conceptual","prompt":"Is the higher credit price in Murabaha the same as interest? Discuss.","expectedStructure":["Juristic views on two prices","Single credit price views","Qur’anic basis (2:275)","Udovitch vs author","What is prohibited"],"keyConcepts":["time value","Ghaban","debt"],"essentialPoints":["Settled before separation","No increase after execution"],"commonMistakes":["Saying any mark-up is Riba"],"topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,142]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e6.4","kind":"short","prompt":"Why is the sale of debt prohibited, and what alternative exists?","expectedStructure":["Gharar/Riba rationale","Al Kali bil Kali","Rollover","Hawalah","Asset-backed Sukuk"],"keyConcepts":["Bai‘ al Dayn","Hawalah"],"essentialPoints":["Face value with recourse only"],"commonMistakes":["Allowing discounting"],"topicId":"t6.10","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.10","pages":[146,147]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e6.5","kind":"viva","prompt":"What options (Khiyar) does a client have in a Murabaha to Purchase Orderer?","expectedStructure":["Defect","Specification/quality","Fitness certificate"],"keyConcepts":["Khiyar al-‘Aib","Khiyar al-Wasf"],"essentialPoints":["May refuse or rescind"],"commonMistakes":["Forgetting the fitness certificate exception"],"topicId":"t6.12","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.12","pages":[150,152]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e6.6","kind":"long","prompt":"Critically examine the permissibility of a credit price higher than the cash price. How is it distinguished from Riba?","expectedStructure":["Jurists on two prices: settle one before separation","Hadith on two bargains in one; Shukani’s ‘Illah","Qur’anic context of 2:275 (Ibn Abi Hatim, Mujahid, Qatadah)","Udovitch’s argument and the author’s reply","No increase after the price becomes debt; rollover prohibited","Conclusion"],"keyConcepts":["Bai‘ Mu’ajjal","two sales in one","time value in pricing","Riba","rollover"],"essentialPoints":["Credit price is for the goods, not time","Seller cannot re-price sold goods","Customary market practice approved by OIC and Shari’ah boards"],"commonMistakes":["Saying any mark-up is Riba","Allowing late-payment increase","Ignoring the one-price condition"],"topicId":"t6.5.3","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5.3","pages":[139,142]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-22","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain the requirements of a valid sale (Bai') contract as set out for Islamic banks, covering the contracting parties, form, subject matter and price.","expectedStructure":["State the requirement of proper offer and acceptance with free mutual consent","Explain that the sale must be prompt/absolute — a sale attributed to a future date or contingent event is void/voidable","Cover the conditions on the object of sale (Ma'qud 'alayh): lawful, pure, existent, owned, valuable, deliverable, specifically known/identified","Cover the condition on price (Thaman): certain and known"],"keyConcepts":["Bai'","Free mutual consent","Ma'qud 'alayh (subject matter)","Thaman (price)"],"essentialPoints":["A valid sale requires proper offer and acceptance and free consent of both seller and buyer, along with legality of the goods and of the medium of exchange; a sale attributed to a future date or made contingent on a future event is void or voidable and must be executed afresh when that date or event actually arrives.","The object of sale (Ma'qud 'alayh) must be pure, lawful (Mubah), of commercial value (Mal-e-Mutaqawam), clean and marketable — commodities such as wine, pork or idols cannot be the valid subject of a sale.","The object must exist at the time of the contract and be owned by the seller (what is not owned cannot be sold), and it must be in the seller's actual or constructive possession, carrying the seller's ownership risk.","The price (Thaman) must be certain (Muta'ayyan) and known (Malum) to both parties at the time of contract; the underlying principles reflected are mutual consent, justice (fulfilment of promise, correct weights/measures, a clear and definite price) and good manners (honesty, leniency, removal of hardship)."],"commonMistakes":["Overlooking that a sale contingent on a future event is void/voidable, not merely unusual — the requirement that a sale be prompt and absolute is a hard condition.","Forgetting that constructive possession (risk and control transferred, even without physical delivery) is sufficient to satisfy the seller's possession requirement."],"topicId":"t6.5","chapter":6,"source":{"chapter":6,"section":"6.5","pages":[133,134,135]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
